hadiths[627]{hadithnumber,text}:
"18151","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Miqdam, he said: One who saw Ali cutting the hand of a man from the joint informed me."
"18152","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Abdullah al-Taymi, from Hibal ibn Rufayda al-Taymi, that Ali used to cut the foot from the sole."
"18153","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Najda ibn Amir wrote to Ibn Abbas: The thief steals and his hand is cut, then he returns, so is his other hand cut? Allah the Exalted said: Cut off their hands; he said: Rather, his hand and his foot alternately; he said: Amr said: I heard it from Ata forty years ago."
"18154","From Ma'mar, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Ali would cut nothing but the hand and the foot, and if he stole after that he would be imprisoned and punished; and he used to say: I am shy before Allah not to leave him a hand with which he eats and cleans himself."
"18155","From Ma'mar, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to say: The son of Adam is not left like a beast, without a hand with which he eats and cleans himself."
"18156","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Simak ibn Harb, from Abd al-Rahman ibn A'idh al-Azdi, from Umar, that he was brought a man who had stolen, called Sadum, and he cut him, then he was brought to him a second time and he cut him, then he was brought to him a third time and he intended to cut him, so Ali said to him: Do not do it; he is only liable for a hand and a foot, but imprison him."
"18157","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Abu al-Duha: That Ali used to say: If he steals, his hand is cut, then if he steals a second time, his foot is cut, and if he steals after that we see no cutting upon him."
"18158","From Ma'mar, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I was present, I saw Umar cut the foot of a man, after a hand and a foot; he had stolen a third time."
"18159","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad: That a thief whose hand and foot had been cut stole jewelry belonging to Asma', and Abu Bakr cut him a third time; he said: I think he said his hand."
"18160","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim and others, he said: Abu Bakr only cut his foot, and he had had his hand cut before that; al-Zuhri said: And nothing has reached us in the Sunna except the cutting of the hand and the foot; nothing is added to that."
"18161","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: Abu Bakr only cut the foot of the one whom Ya'la ibn Umayya had cut, and he had already had his hand cut before that."
"18162","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: When the thief steals, his hand is cut; then if he steals a second time, his foot is cut; then if he steals a third time, his hand is cut; then if he steals a fourth time, his foot is cut."
"18163","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd Rabbih ibn Abi Umayya informed me that al-Harith ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Rabi'a narrated to him that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was brought a slave who stole, and he was brought four times, and he let him go; then he was brought a fifth time and he cut his hand, then a sixth time and he cut his foot, then a seventh time and he cut his hand, then an eighth time and he cut his foot."
"18164","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa, from Aisha, she said: There was a black man who used to come to Abu Bakr, and he would draw him near and teach him the Qur'an, until he sent out a collector, or he said: an expedition, and he said: Send me with him; and he said: Rather you shall stay with us; but he refused, so he sent him with him and commended him well; and he was not absent from him but a little until he came with his hand cut; and when Abu Bakr saw him his eyes overflowed, and he said: What is your affair? He said: I did no more than that he entrusted to me something of his work, and I betrayed a single obligatory due, so he cut my hand; so Abu Bakr said: You find the one who cut the hand of this man betraying more than twenty obligatory dues; by Allah, if you are truthful I shall surely retaliate for you against him; he said: Then he drew him near and did not change the rank he had with him; he said: And the man used to stand at night and recite, and when Abu Bakr heard his voice he would say: By Allah, the night of one whom this one has cut; he said: And only a little passed until the family of Abu Bakr missed jewelry and goods of theirs, and Abu Bakr said: The quarter was visited by night; so the one with the stump stood facing the qibla and raised his sound hand and the other that had been cut, and said: O Allah, expose the one who stole from them, or the like of this; and Ma'mar would sometimes say: O Allah, expose the one who stole from the people of this righteous house; he said: And midday had not come until they found the goods with him; so Abu Bakr said to him: Woe to you, how little is your knowledge of Allah; then he ordered concerning him and his foot was cut; Ma'mar said: And Ayyub informed me, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, the like of it, except that he said: When Abu Bakr heard his voice at night he would say: Your night is not the night of a thief."
"18165","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: More than one of the people of Medina informed me, among them Isma'il ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'd, that Ya'la cut the hand and the foot of the thief, then he stole a third time, and Abu Bakr cut his second hand; then he mentioned the like of the hadith of al-Zuhri; he said: And Abu Bakr used to say: His boldness against Allah angers me more than his theft; Ibn Jurayj said: And Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr informed me that his name is Jabr or Jubayr."
"18166","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: Concerning a man with a withered hand who stole, he said: His hand is cut even if it is withered."
"18167","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi: That he was asked about a thief brought forward to be cut, and he put forward his left, and it was cut; he said: He is left alone and nothing is added to that."
"18168","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, the like of the saying of al-Sha'bi: Nothing is added to that; the hadd has been carried out upon him."
"18169","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from Ikrima ibn Khalid, he said: Ali would not cut a thief until he brought the witnesses and confronted them with him and imprisoned him; then if they testified against him he cut him, and if they held back he left him; he said: And once he was brought a thief, and he imprisoned him, until on the morrow he summoned him and the two witnesses, and it was said: The two witnesses have disappeared; so he released the thief and did not cut him."
"18170","From Ma'mar, from Qatada: Concerning a man against whom one man testified that he stole in one land, and another testified against him that he stole in another land, he said: There is no cutting upon him."
"18171","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: Concerning a man found stealing, and he confessed that he had stolen before that, he said: His hand is cut; nothing is added to that."
"18172","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If he stole and then stole again and was not punished, he is cut a single time, and likewise the adulterer; and Ibn Shihab said the like."
"18173","From al-Thawri, from Jabir and al-A'mash, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, he said: A man came to Ali and said: I have stolen, and he sent him back; then he said: I have stolen, and he said: You have testified against yourself twice; then he cut him; he said: And I saw his hand hung upon his neck."
"18174","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, that a man came to Ali and said: I have stolen; so he rebuked him and reviled him; then he said: I have stolen; so Ali said: Cut him; he has testified against himself twice; and I indeed saw it upon his neck."
"18175","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said to him: A man testified against himself a single time; he said: It suffices him."
"18176","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Confession is not allowed after punishment, in a hadd or otherwise."
"18177","From Sufyan, he said: When he confesses a theft and then denies before the ruler, if he holds back he is left alone, and he pays what he confessed, and he is not cut; or he stole and then died before he was cut, the theft is taken from his wealth if the hadd was not carried out upon him, and the wealth is not lost."
"18178","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maysara, that a man was with a people accused of partisanship, and one morning he was found slain, and a man of the people was accused of him; so Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz sent for him and ordered the whips; and the man said: O Muslims! By Allah, I have not killed him, and if he flogs me I shall surely confess; so Umar ordered concerning him that he be made to swear, and he released him."
"18179","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Some people frightened a boy until he confessed to them some of what they wanted, then he denied afterwards, and they disputed with him to Shurayh; and he said: It is this: if he wishes, he confesses; and he did not allow his confession under threat."
"18180","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Torture is an innovation."
"18181","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Shurayh, he said: The fetter is coercion, and the threat is coercion, and imprisonment is coercion, and beating is coercion."
"18182","From al-Thawri, from al-Shaybani, from Hanzala, from his father, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: A man is not trustworthy concerning himself when you starve him, or bind him, or beat him."
"18183","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from Ikrima ibn Khalid, that Umar ibn al-Khattab was brought a thief and he confessed; he said: I see the hand of a man, it is not the hand of a thief; so the man said: By Allah, I am not a thief, but they threatened me; so he released him and did not cut him."
"18184","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: Concerning a man who sold a free man, and said: The price is between me and you, he said: They are both punished, and the price is returned to the one who bought him; Ma'mar said: And one who heard al-Hasan say it informed me of it."
"18185","From Sufyan: Concerning the man who sells a free man, he said: There is no cutting upon him, and he has no sale, and upon him is discretionary punishment."
"18186","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: He becomes a slave as he acknowledged slavery upon himself; Qatada said: And Ali said: He does not become a slave, and the seller is cut."
"18187","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: That a man sold his daughter and the buyer had intercourse with her, and her father said: Need drove me to sell her; he said: The father and the slave-girl are flogged a hundred each, if the slave-girl has reached puberty, and the price is returned to the buyer, and upon the buyer is her dower for what he took of her, then the father pays it to him on his behalf, unless the buyer knew that she was free, in which case the dower is upon him and the father does not pay it for him, and upon him are a hundred lashes; and if she is a girl without understanding, then the exemplary punishment is upon the father."
"18188","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Free men are not sold, nor given away as charity."
"18189","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Free men are not sold."
"18190","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata about a man who acknowledged that he is a slave; he said: A free man does not become a slave."
"18191","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: I said to him: A free man acknowledged slavery, and was then pawned; he said: He is a pledge until he ransoms himself, as he deceived them."
"18192","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: I asked him about a man who stole a non-Arabic-speaking slave who does not understand; he said: His hand is cut."
"18193","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il, from al-Hasan, he said: Whoever steals a minor, free or slave, therein is cutting; he said: And Ibrahim said: The hadd is carried out upon the adult, and there is nothing upon the minor."
"18194","From Sufyan, he says: Whatever is stolen of a minor owned slave, therein is cutting; and whoever steals of a minor, free or an owned one who has grown up, there is no cutting upon him; Sufyan said: When a man sells his wife, and the buyer has intercourse with her, and she bears a child, then it is known afterwards? He said: She is returned to her husband, and it is not a separation, and the woman and her husband are disciplined."
"18195","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Ibn Shubruma, he said: Yusuf ibn Umar summoned me and asked me about a man who sold his wife: Is cutting upon him? He said: I said: No; it has reached us that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said in the Farewell Pilgrimage: You have taken them only by the trust of Allah, so she is with us a trust; he betrayed it; there is no cutting upon him; he said: So he beat him a beating that was harsher upon him than cutting."
"18196","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed that Ali cut the seller, and said: A free man does not become a slave; he said: And Ibn Abbas said: There is no cutting upon him, and upon him is the like of cutting: imprisonment."
"18197","From Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah, that Amr ibn Sulaym, their freedman, informed him that Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab was asked about a man who sells his child; he said: If he sold one who has reached understanding, and he acknowledged that, then upon the woman, if she is approached, is the hadd, and upon her father is the painful punishment, and the payment of her price is upon her father, and her child is in the position of a lawful child; and if he is a man who has reached understanding, then upon him and upon his father is the painful punishment, and upon his father is the payment of his price."
"18198","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, that he cut a man for a slave-boy he stole."
"18199","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The thief is found in the house, having gathered the goods but not gone out with them; he said: There is no cutting upon him until he goes out with them; he said: And Amr ibn Dinar said to me: I see no cutting upon him."
"18200","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, that Uthman ruled that there is no cutting upon him, even if he gathered the goods and intended to steal, until he removes them and goes out with them."
"18201","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, that a thief bored through the strong-room of al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wada'a, and was found in it having gathered the goods but not gone out with them; so he was brought to Ibn al-Zubayr, and he flogged him and ordered that he be cut; then Ibn Umar passed by and asked, and was informed, and he came to Ibn al-Zubayr and said: Did you order that he be cut? He said: Yes; he said: Then what is the matter of the flogging? He said: Ibn al-Zubayr said: I was angered; Ibn Umar said: And there is no cutting upon him until he goes out with them from the house; tell me, if you saw a man between the legs of a woman and he did not touch her, would you punish him? He said: No; he said: Perhaps he will repent before he commits it; he said: And this is likewise; what do you know, perhaps he had desisted and repented and abandoned the goods."
"18202","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: When the thief is found in the house having gathered the goods in the house and not gone out with them, there is no cutting upon him, but he is punished exemplarily."
"18203","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, from one of the rulers, he said: He is not cut; he is a man who intended to steal and they did not let him."
"18204","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: When he gathers the goods and goes out with them from the house into the courtyard, cutting is upon him."
"18205","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Abi al-Safar, from al-Sha'bi, he said: The thief is not cut until he goes out with the goods from the house; and its interpretation with us is: so long as it is in the property of the man, there is no cutting upon him; from al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, the like of the saying of al-Sha'bi."
"18206","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim, from Husayn ibn Abdullah ibn Dumayra, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ali, he said: The hand of the thief is not cut until he brings the goods out of the house."
"18207","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, he said: Ibn Umar found a thief in his house, and he came out against him with a drawn sword, and he kept turning about while he held it back from him; he said: And had we not restrained him he would have struck him with it."
"18208","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah informed me, Abd al-Razzaq said: And I asked Abu Bakr about it and he informed me of it, that Khalid ibn Sa'id narrated to him, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab and Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba, that they were both asked about: The thief steals, then he throws away the theft, and is found in the house he steals from, not having gone out? And they both said: Cutting is upon him."
"18209","From Ma'mar, from Khusayf al-Jazari, he said: Some people missed goods of theirs from their house, and saw a breach in the house, and went out to look, and there were two men hurrying; so they caught one of them with their goods with him, and the other escaped them; he said: So we brought him, and he said: I did not steal; this one, meaning the one who escaped them, only hired me and handed me these goods to carry them; I do not know whence he brought them; Khusayf said: So we wrote concerning him to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, and he ordered us to punish him exemplarily and to imprison him for life, and not to cut him."
"18210","From al-Hajjaj, from Husayn, from al-Sha'bi, from al-Harith, he said: Ali was brought a man who bored through a house, and he did not cut him, and he disciplined him with lashes."
"18211","From Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali: That he was brought a man who bored through a house, and he did not cut him."
"18212","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: Concerning the man with whom goods are found, and their owners recognize them, and he says: I bought them, he said: There is no cutting upon him, but if he is accused his affair is investigated; then if it is exposed against him he is cut, and the goods are returned to their owners; and Qatada said likewise except his saying: His affair is investigated."
"18213","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, he said: I heard him say: Do you testify that it is his goods? You do not know that he sold, nor gave; then he takes his oath by Allah: I have not sold, nor given, nor destroyed, nor rendered to be destroyed; then his goods are returned to him, unless the other brings a matter establishing by which he deserves."
"18214","From Isra'il, from Simak ibn Harb, from Hajjaj ibn Abjar, he said: I witnessed Ali when he was brought a man from whom a garment was stolen, and he found it with the thief, and he established proof upon him; so Ali said: Hand over to this one his garment, and you pursue the one from whom you bought; and Jabir informed me, from Amir, from Ali, that he judged likewise."
"18215","From al-Thawri, concerning a man who bought a slave and traveled with him, and the slave was recognized with him as stolen, he said: I judge against him, and I refer him to the one from whom he bought."
"18216","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: A man borrowed goods then sold them, and the man found his goods with the one who bought them, and he disputed concerning them with Anas ibn Sirin to a judge who was in Basra called Umayra ibn Yathribi, and he said to Anas: Seek your companion to whom you lent."
"18217","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man stole my wealth, and I found that he sold it; he said: Then take it wherever you find it; I said: And I entrusted him with it, and he betrayed it and sold it; he said: Take it wherever you find it; glory be to Allah, it is nothing but that; I said: Then he borrowed it from me and sold it; he said: And likewise, then take it; he said: I said: Then a man stole a slave of mine, and gave her as dower to a woman and had intercourse with her; he said: We have heard it said: Take your wealth wherever you find it, so take your slave from her."
"18218","He said: And Ikrima ibn Khalid informed me that Usayd ibn Zuhayr al-Ansari informed him that he was a governor over Yamama, and that Marwan wrote to him that Mu'awiya wrote to me: Whatever man has a theft stolen from him, he has most right to it wherever he finds it; he said: And Marwan wrote that to me, so I wrote to Marwan that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, judged that when the one who bought it from the one who stole it is not accused, its master is given choice: if he wishes he takes what was stolen from him for its price, and if he wishes he pursues his thief; then Abu Bakr and Umar and Uthman judged with that afterwards; he said: So Marwan sent my letter to Mu'awiya; he said: So Mu'awiya wrote to Marwan: You are not, neither you nor Usayd ibn Zuhayr, judges over me; but I judge in what I am placed over you both, so execute what I ordered you; so Marwan sent to me the letter of Mu'awiya, and I said: I shall not judge with it so long as I am placed, meaning by the saying of Mu'awiya."
"18219","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa, from Aisha, she said: There was a Makhzumi woman who would borrow goods and deny them, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered her hand to be cut; then her people came to Usama ibn Zayd and spoke to him, and Usama spoke to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, concerning her; so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: O Usama, you keep interceding concerning a hadd of the hudud of Allah; then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stood as a preacher and said: Those before you were destroyed only because when the noble among them stole they left him, and when the weak among them stole they cut him; by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, had Fatima the daughter of Muhammad been, I would have cut her hand."
"18220","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, he said: Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ali informed me, he said: A woman stole; Amr said: I thought he said: Of the daughters of the Ka'ba; so she was brought to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and Umar ibn Abi Salama came and said to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: She is my aunt; so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Had Fatima the daughter of Muhammad been, I would have cut her hand; Amr said: So I had no doubt when Hasan said: Umar said to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: She is my aunt, that she is the daughter of al-Aswad ibn Abd al-Asad, the daughter of the brother of Sufyan ibn Abd al-Asad; Amr ibn Dinar said: And Ikrima ibn Khalid informed me, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith, he said: The daughter of al-Aswad ibn Abd al-Asad borrowed something falsely and concealed it, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, cut her; he said: I thought, of Fatima."
"18221","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I think Ikrima ibn Khalid informed me that Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith informed him that a woman came to a woman and said: So-and-so woman asks to borrow from you jewelry, and she is lying; so she lent it to her; then days passed and she did not see her jewelry; then the one who had lied came on her behalf and asked her for her jewelry, and she said: I have not borrowed anything from you; so she returned to the other and asked her for her jewelry, and she denied that she had borrowed anything from her; so she came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he summoned her, and she said: By the One Who sent you with the truth, I have not borrowed anything from her; so he said: Go and take it from beneath her bedding; so she was cut; and the people disliked sheltering her, so he said: We have carried out what is upon her, so whoever wishes, let him shelter her; Ibn Jurayj said: And Bishr ibn Taym informed me that she is Umm Amr, daughter of Sufyan ibn Abd al-Asad; he said: I find none besides her, meaning: I know this lineage only in her."
"18222","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id informed me that he heard Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab say: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was brought a woman in a great house of the houses of Quraysh, who had come to people and said: The family of so-and-so ask to borrow from you such-and-such; so they lent her; then they came to those and they denied that they had borrowed from them, and she denied that she had borrowed from them; so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, cut her."
"18223","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn al-Munkadir, he said: The wife of Usayd ibn Hudayr sheltered her, and Usayd came, and she mentioned her to him, and he blamed her and said: I shall not lay down my garment until I come to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him; so he came to him and mentioned that to him, and he said: She showed her mercy, may Allah have mercy upon her."
"18224","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, cut the hand of a man; then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, passed by him while a man had built for him a tent in which he shaded himself; so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Who sheltered this afflicted one? They said: Atik sheltered him, or Ibn Atik; so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: O Allah, bless Atik and the family of Atik, as they sheltered this afflicted servant of Yours."
"18225","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: If a man borrows from a man goods falsely on behalf of a man and conceals them, he said: He is not cut, they claimed."
"18226","From Ma'mar, from one of his companions, from al-Hakam ibn Utayba, concerning a slave-girl who borrowed jewelry in the names of her masters, then she absconded, and her masters said: We did not order her with anything, he said: When the one whom the slave-girl took from cannot be found, then the jewelry is upon the neck of the slave-girl."
"18227","From al-Thawri: Concerning the one who borrows goods, then denies them before a judge, then proof is established, he is seized with them; and when he denies them before people it is nothing; and the one who borrows in the name of a man, there is no cutting upon him therein."
"18228","From al-Thawri: Concerning a slave-girl who borrows in the names of her masters, he said: There is nothing upon the slave-girl, nor upon her masters, because those who gave her wasted them."
"18229","From Hisham, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered a camel and it was slaughtered, and the people plundered its meat; so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent a caller saying: Allah and His Messenger forbid you plundering; so they returned it, and he divided it among them."
"18230","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, he said: Simak ibn Harb informed us, from Tha'laba ibn al-Hakam, he said: We seized sheep on the day of Khaybar, and the people plundered them; so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came while their pots were boiling, and said: What is this? And they said: Plunder, O Messenger of Allah! He said: Overturn them, for plunder is not lawful; so they overturned what remained in them."
"18231","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered a camel and it was slaughtered, and the people plundered its meat; so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered a caller and he called: Allah and His Messenger forbid you plundering."
"18232","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Amr ibn Shu'ayb say: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever plunders a notable plunder, or shelters an innovator in Islam, or takes as master the freedman of a people without their permission, upon him is the curse of Allah, with no turning away from it and no requital."
"18233","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr said to me, he said: Jabir ibn Abdullah said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: There is no cutting upon the plunderer, and whoever plunders a well-known plunder is not of us, not like us; Ibn Jurayj said it, from Yasin, that he heard Abu al-Zubayr narrating from Jabir ibn Abdullah, from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the like of it."
"18234","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim Abu Umayya informed me, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever commits an offense therein, or shelters an offender, or takes as master the freedman of a people without their permission, upon him is the curse of Allah, with no turning away from it and no requital; he said: And Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf said: And what is the offense, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Whoever plunders a plunder to which the people raise their eyes, or mutilates without a hadd, or institutes a practice that did not exist; I said to Abd al-Karim: His saying, whoever commits an offense therein? He said: The sacred Mecca; and others added, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: Or kills without right."
"18235","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ja'far ibn Muhammad informed us, from his father, from his grandfather, that there was found with the sword of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, a document hung upon the hilt of the sword containing: The most insolent of people against Allah is the killer of other than his killer, and the striker of other than his striker, and whoever shelters an offender, there shall not be accepted from him on the Day of Resurrection any turning away nor requital, and whoever takes as master other than his master has disbelieved in what was sent down upon Muhammad; I said to Ja'far: The one who shelters an offender, the one who kills? He said: Yes."
"18236","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever commits an offense, or shelters an offender, upon him is the curse of Allah and the angels and all people; Ma'mar said: And Ja'far ibn Muhammad said: It was said: O Messenger of Allah! What is the offender? He said: Whoever flogs without a hadd, or kills without right."
"18237","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: If a man snatches the goods of a man? He said: He is not cut; and Amr ibn Dinar said it to me."
"18238","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: A man snatched goods, and Marwan wanted to cut his hand, so Zayd ibn Thabit said to him: That is open snatching; there is no cutting in it, but exemplary punishment and chastisement."
"18239","From al-Thawri, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ibn Ubayd ibn al-Abras, and he is Zayd ibn Dithar, he said: A man snatched a garment and was brought to Ali, and he said: I was only playing with him; so he said: Did you know him? He said: Yes; so he released him."
"18240","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il ibn Muslim, from al-Hasan, from Ali, he said: He was asked about snatching, and he said: That is open wickedness; there is no cutting in it."
"18241","From Ma’mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: There is no cutting in it; cutting is only in what ... ... ."
"18242","From Ma’mar, from Ayyub, he said: Iyas ibn Mu’awiya wrote to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz concerning three judgments, among them the snatcher; he said: And Iyas read to me the letter when it came to him, and in it was that the snatcher be punished and imprisoned for life, in prison."
"18243","From Ma’mar, from Simak ibn al-Fadl, he said: Ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to Urwa in Yemen: That which is taken openly by snatching, there is no cutting in it; cutting is only in what is taken from behind a lock secretly, in which there is neither snatching nor openness."
"18244","From Ma’mar, from Qatada, he said: There is no cutting upon the snatcher, but he is imprisoned and punished."
"18245","From Hushaym ibn Bashir, from Abdullah ibn Sabra al-Hamdani, from al-Sha’bi, he said: There is no cutting upon the snatcher."
"18246","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: There is no cutting upon the snatcher."
"18247","From Yasin, that Abu al-Zubayr informed him, from Jabir, he said: There is no cutting upon the traitor, nor upon the plunderer, nor upon the snatcher; I said: From the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him? He said: Then from whom?"
"18248","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: There is no cutting upon the traitor."
"18249","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Treachery? He said: There is no cutting in it, nor any known hadd; Ibn Jurayj said: And Amr ibn Dinar said to me: Nothing of it has reached me."
"18250","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Isma’il ibn Muslim informed me that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq said: Concerning treachery, there is no cutting in it."
"18251","From Ma’mar, he said: It has reached me that in treachery there is exemplary punishment."
"18252","From Ma’mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: There is no cutting upon the traitor."
"18253","He said: And al-Zuhri was asked about a man who stayed as guest with a people and betrayed them, and he saw no cutting upon him."
"18254","From Ma’mar, from al-Zuhri, from al-Sa’ib ibn Yazid, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab when Abdullah ibn Amr al-Hadrami came to him with a slave-boy of his, and said to him: This slave-boy of mine has stolen, so cut his hand; so Umar said: What did he steal? He said: The mirror of my wife, its value sixty dirhams; he said: Release him, there is no cutting upon him; your servant took your goods; but had he stolen from other than you he would have been cut."
"18255","From Ma’mar, from al-A’mash, from Ibrahim, that Ma’qil ibn Muqarrin asked Ibn Mas’ud and said: A slave of mine stole from a slave of mine? He said: Cut him; then he said: No, your wealth took your wealth; he said: My slave-girl committed adultery; he said: Flog her fifty."
"18256","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, that Ma’qil ibn Muqarrin asked Ibn Mas’ud, he said: A lad of mine stole something from a lad of mine; is cutting upon him? He said: No, your wealth is partly within partly."
"18257","From Ma’mar, he said: The slave is not cut by the testimony of his master alone."
"18258","From al-Thawri, he said: If the mukatab steals something from his master, he is not cut; and if the master steals something from the mukatab, he is not cut."
"18259","From al-Thawri, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ibn Ubayd ibn al-Abras, and he is Zayd ibn Dithar, he said: Ali was brought a man who stole from the khums, and he said: He has a share in it; it is allowed; so he did not cut him; he stole a helmet."
"18260","From Ma’mar, from Mughira, from al-Sha’bi, he said: One who steals from the public treasury is not cut, because he has a share in it."
"18261","From Abdullah ibn Muharrar, he said: Maymun ibn Mihran informed me, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was brought a slave who had stolen from the khums, and he said: The wealth of Allah, part of it stole part; there is no cutting upon him."
"18262","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhriz ibn al-Qasim informed me, from more than one trustworthy one, that a man attacked the public treasury of Kufa and stole it, and Ibn Mas’ud resolved to cut him, and he wrote to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and Umar wrote: Do not cut him, for he has a right in it."
"18263","From Ma’mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: I heard him say concerning one who steals the graves of the dead, he said: Marwan seized them in Medina and punished them a painful exemplary punishment, and paraded them, and forbade them, and did not cut them."
"18264","From Ma’mar, from Qatada, he said: When they are found after digging up graves and taking their garments, their hands are cut."
"18265","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Nothing has reached me concerning the grave-robber."
"18266","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar said to me, he said: Abbad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr cut the hand and the foot of a lad who robbed graves."
"18267","Ibn Jurayj said: And it has reached me from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz that he said: One who steals our living and our dead is alike."
"18268","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: When the grave-digger steals what would be cut for the like of, he is cut."
"18269","From al-Thawri, from Umar ibn Ayyub, he said: I heard al-Sha’bi say: We cut for our dead as we cut for our living; Sufyan said: And what is most beloved to us is: There is no cutting upon them, but exemplary punishment."
"18270","From al-Thawri, from Ja’far ibn Burqan, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz used to say: In it is cutting; and al-Thawri does not adopt it."
"18271","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, he said: Yahya al-Ghassani informed me, he said: I wrote to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz about the grave robber, and he wrote back to me that he is a thief."
"18272","From al-Thawri, he said: We do not hold there is amputation for the grave robber, even if he carries it off to his house, for he is in the position of dirhams buried in the earth; we do not hold there is amputation for taking them out. But if the grave robber takes any of the garments, he is disciplined and fined."
"18273","From Ibn Jurayj, from Safwan ibn Sulaym: that a man from among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) found a man digging up graves, so he killed him, and Umar declared his blood void."
"18274","From Ibrahim, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, he said: A man died in Madinah, and his brother feared his grave would be robbed, so he guarded it. The robber came, and he kept quiet about him until he brought out the shrouds, then he came to him and struck him with the sword until he grew cold. That was raised to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he declared his blood void."
"18275","From Ibrahim, he said: Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr informed me, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah: that he found people robbing graves in Yemen in the time of Umar ibn al-Khattab, so he wrote to Umar, and Umar wrote to him to cut off their hands."
"18276","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed, from Amrah bint Abd al-Rahman, from Aishah, that she said: The male grave robber and the female grave robber are cursed."
"18277","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, he said: Al-Shabi was brought a qaffaf, so he struck him with lashes and released him. He said: And the qaffaf is the one who weighs dirhams and steals from them."
"18278","From al-Thawri: From their companions, about the tarrar there is amputation upon him, because it is tied up, and it is in the position of a house. And the tarrar is the one who steals tied-up dirhams."
"18279","From Mamar, from Bahz ibn Hakim ibn Muawiyah, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seized people of my tribe on suspicion and detained them. Then a man of my tribe came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was preaching and said: O Muhammad, for what are you detaining my neighbors. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept silent from him. Then he said: People say you forbid evil and keep to it in private. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is he saying. So I began to interpose speech between them, fearing he would hear it and supplicate against my tribe with a supplication after which they would never prosper. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept on until he understood it, then said: Have they said it. And its sayer was among them. By Allah, if I did it, it would be against me and not against them. Release his neighbors to him."
"18280","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yahya ibn Said informed me, from Irak ibn Malik, he said: Two men of Banu Ghifar came until they halted at a halting place at Dajnan, of the waters of Madinah, and with it were people of Ghatafan with riding animals of theirs. In the morning the Ghatafanis had lost two paired camels of theirs, so they accused the two Ghifaris. They brought them to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned their affair to him. He detained one of the two Ghifaris and said to the other: Go and seek them. It was only a little while until he came with them. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to one of the two Ghifaris, he said: I thought he said the one detained with him: Seek forgiveness for me. He said: May Allah forgive you, O Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And for you, and may He kill you in His path. He said: So he was killed on the day of Yamamah."
"18281","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Abi Mulaykah saying: Abdullah ibn Abi Amir informed me, he said: I set out in a caravan, until when we came to Dhu al-Marwah a baggage case of mine was stolen, and with us was a man suspected. My companions said: O so-and-so, render his baggage case. He said: I did not take it. So I returned to Umar ibn al-Khattab and informed him. He said: How many are you. So I counted them. He said: I think it is its owner, the one suspected. I said: I wished, O Commander of the Faithful, to bring him to you bound. He said: Would you bring him bound without proof. I shall not write for you about it, nor ask for you about it. He said: So he grew angry. He said: So he did not write for me about it, nor ask about it."
"18282","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If you find stolen goods with a bad man who is suspected, and he says: I bought it, but he does not produce from whom he bought it, or he says: I took it, he is not amputated and not punished. And Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdullah with a letter I read: that when goods are found with a suspect man and he says: I bought it, but he does not produce it, then bind him firmly in prison and do not release him at the word of anyone until the command of Allah comes. So I mentioned that to Ata, and he disapproved of it."
"18283","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Bakr informed me, from Ibn Sirin, he said: I witnessed Shurayh when they were brought to him with the theft with them, and he would say: I bought it. So Shurayh would say: You have exposed the theft and concealed the thief. So that would be uncovered with a severe uncovering, and he did not amputate in it."
"18284","From Sufyan, about a man and two women who testified against a man that he stole a garment worth twenty dirhams, he said: We allow their testimony for the property, and we do not amputate him."
"18285","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, about the thief, he said: Amputation suffices him, and even if he is wealthy he does not pay with amputation, unless the theft itself is found with him, then it is taken from him."
"18286","From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from al-Shabi, he said: There is no fine upon the thief unless something itself is found when he is cut."
"18287","From Hushaym, from Ashath, from Ibn Sirin, he said: When the theft is found with the thief it is taken from him, and when it is not found with him his hand is cut and there is no liability upon him."
"18288","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, he said: It is a debt upon the thief; his hand is cut and it is taken from him. Sufyan said: And the saying of al-Shabi is more beloved to me."
"18289","From Mamar, he said: We heard that the thief with whom his theft is found is cut, and the goods are returned to their owners; we did not hear of a fine in it when the goods are not found with him."
"18290","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri: About a man who killed a man and took his wealth, he said: He is killed for him and fined the like of the wealth he took from him. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab said the like of that."
"18291","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Whoever steals wine from the People of the Book is cut. Ata said: They claimed about wine and pork flesh stolen by the Muslim from the People of the Book, he is cut because it is lawful for them in their religion; but if he steals that from a Muslim, there is no cutting."
"18292","From Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ata, he said: Whoever steals wine from the People of the Book is cut, and if he steals from the Muslims he is not cut."
"18293","From al-Thawri, he said: There is no cutting upon one who steals wine from the People of the Book, but he pays its price. He said: And Ibn Abi Najih said, from Ata: He is cut."
"18294","From Ibn al-Mubarak, from al-Thawri, from Jabir al-Jufi, from Abdullah ibn Kaysan, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz intended to cut a man who stole a chicken, so Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman said to him: Uthman ibn Affan would not cut for birds. Al-Thawri said: And it is deemed good that one who steals from one of mahram kin is not cut, his maternal uncle or paternal uncle or one of mahram kin."
"18295","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It reached me from Amir, he said: There is no cutting upon the husband of the woman for stealing her goods. Ibn Jurayj said: And Abd al-Karim said: There is no cutting upon the woman for stealing his goods. He said: And in treachery from this there is clarification."
"18296","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb and others he approves of, they said: There is no cutting for feathers, even if its price is a dinar or more, meaning birds and what resembles them."
"18297","From al-Thawri: About a man who cuts off ten hands, he said: He says: Whoever of you agrees that his hand be cut, we cut it, and the rest take the blood money. If some of them take the blood money, both his hands are cut for those who wanted retaliation, and what remains is a debt upon him for the rest of them. And if they refuse but retaliation, he is cut for all of them, and what remains of the blood money is between them both together."
"18298","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Two hands are not cut for one hand."
"18299","From Mamar: About a man who steals goods from a man, then another comes and steals it from the thief, he said: The first thief is cut, and as for the one who stole it from the thief, there is no cutting upon him, but upon him is the fine. From Ibn al-Mubarak, from al-Thawri, the like of the saying of Mamar, except that al-Thawri said: Upon him is the fine of what he took."
"18300","From Said ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Hilal ibn Sad: that a man entered the bathhouse and left a cloak of his, then a man came and stole it. Its owner found him and brought him to Abu al-Darda and said: Establish the hadd of Allah upon this one. Abu al-Darda said: Malik ibn Adi informed us, I seek refuge in Allah from you. He said: Shall I leave him. He said: Yes, leave him, meaning that the bathhouse thief is not cut."
"18301","From al-Thawri, from a man, from al-Hasan, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a thief who stole food, and he did not cut him. Sufyan said: And it is that which spoils in its day and has no lasting, tharid and meat and what resembles them, so there is no cutting in it, but he is disciplined. And when the fruit is on its tree there is no cutting in it, but he is disciplined."
"18302","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yahya ibn Said informed me that Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban informed him, from a man, from Rafi ibn Khadij, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There is no cutting for fruit nor for kathr."
"18303","From Muhammad, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir: that Rafi ibn Khadij said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no cutting for fruit nor for kathr. And kathr is the jummar that is in the palm; when the jummar is removed the palm dies."
"18304","From Muhammad, from Ata al-Khurasani, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Whoever takes anything of fruit, there is no cutting upon him until he shelters it to the drying places and threshing floors. If he takes from it thereafter what equals a quarter dinar, he is cut. And marabid are also the jarin."
"18305","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ata saying: Those of the past, when one of them was brought the thief, he would say: Did you steal. Say: No. Did you steal. Say: No. To my knowledge he named Abu Bakr and Umar. And he informed me: that Ali was brought two thieves with their theft with them, so he went out and struck the people with the stick until they dispersed from them, and he did not summon them nor ask about them."
"18306","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab was brought a man, so he asked him: Did you steal. Say: No. So he said: No. So he left him and did not cut him."
"18307","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Abu Masud al-Ansari: that he was brought a woman who stole a camel, so he said: Did you steal. Say: No."
"18308","From al-Thawri, from Ali ibn al-Aqmar, from Yazid ibn Abi Kabshah, from Abu al-Darda, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him: that he was brought a woman who stole, called Salamah, so he said to her: O Salamah, did you steal. Say: No. She said: No. So he warded off from her."
"18309","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Khusayfah informed me that he heard Ibn Thawban saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a thief who stole a shawl. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, this is a thief. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I do not think he stole. Did you steal, woe to you. He said: Yes. He said: Cut his hand, then cauterize it, then bring him to me. So that was done. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Repent to Allah. He said: I repent to Allah. He said: O Allah, accept his repentance. From al-Thawri, from Yazid ibn Khusayfah, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"18310","From Mamar, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut a thief, then ordered him to be cauterized, then said: Repent to Allah. He said: I repent to Allah. He said: O Allah, accept his repentance. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the thief is cut, his hand falls into the Fire; if he returns it follows it, and if he repents he retrieves it, meaning: he takes it back."
"18311","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri: that Safwan brought the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the thief of his cloak. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered his hand to be cut. He said: I did not intend this, O Messenger of Allah, it is charity upon him. He said: Why not before you brought him."
"18312","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Urwah ibn al-Zubayr saying: Furafisah ibn Umayr al-Hanafi ibn Abd al-Dar informed me that a thief was taken with his theft taken from him. He said: So we seized him and people crowded about him. Zubayr came and said: What is this. So we informed him. He said: Pardon him. We said: O Abu Abdullah, do you speak about a thief with his theft with him. He said: Yes, pardon him; so long as his ruling has not reached, then when his ruling is reached it is not lawful for him to leave him, nor for an intercessor to intercede for him."
"18313","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah: that al-Furafisah, Zubayr passed by him while he had seized a thief, and with him people, so he interceded for him. Al-Furafisah said: We convey him to the commander; if he wills he pardons him. Zubayr said: If the commander pardons him, may Allah not pardon him."
"18314","From Ayyub, from Ikrimah: that Ammar ibn Yasir seized a thief, then said: I cover him; perhaps Allah will cover me."
"18315","From al-Thawri, he said: My father informed me, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas: that he seized a thief, then provisioned him and sent him off. And that Ammar seized the thief of his baggage case, and he was led to him, but he did not revile him and left him."
"18316","From Ibrahim ibn Tahman, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq said: If I found for the thief, the adulterer and the wine drinker naught but my garment, I would love to cover him with it."
"18317","From al-Thawri, from Muttarih, from al-Hasan, he said: Umar said: Evade the thief and do not frighten him. He says: Drive him away, shout at him, and do not lie in wait for him."
"18318","From Mamar, from Muhammad ibn Wasi, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever covers a Muslim, Allah covers him in the Hereafter; whoever relieves a Muslim of a distress, Allah relieves him of a distress in the Hereafter; and Allah is in the aid of the Muslim so long as he is in the aid of his brother."
"18319","From Mamar, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: I do not know whether he raised it or not, he said: Whoever covers a Muslim, Allah covers him."
"18320","Muhammad ibn Rashid informed us, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa informed us, from one who narrated to him, from a man of the Ansar from among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that he went out from Madinah to Uqbah ibn Amir, while he was governor over Egypt, to ask him about a hadith they both heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) together. So he asked him about it. Uqbah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever covers his brother for an indecency he sees upon him, Allah covers him in the world and the Hereafter. Sulayman said: And Uthman in his rule was called to people over an ugly matter, so he went to them in the evening, but did not find them together, and saw an ugly matter; so he praised Allah since he did not find them together, and freed a slave."
"18321","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn al-Munkadir, from Abu Ayyub, and from Maslamah ibn Makhlad: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever covers a Muslim, Allah covers him in the world and the Hereafter; whoever delivers one in distress, Allah frees him from a distress of the distresses of the Day of Resurrection; and whoever is in the need of his brother, Allah is in his need."
"18322","Ibn Jurayj said: And Abu Ayyub rode to Uqbah ibn Amir, in Egypt, and said: I ask you about a matter none who attended it remains but I and you: How did you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever covers a believer in the world over a private fault, Allah covers him on the Day of Resurrection. Then he returned to Madinah and did not untie his baggage. Abu Said narrates this hadith to Ata."
"18323","From Ibn Jurayj and al-Muthanna, they said: Amr ibn Shuayb informed us, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pardon among yourselves before you bring to me; whatever hadd reaches me has become obligatory."
"18324","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that they said to Safwan ibn Umayyah ibn Khalaf after the conquest: There is no religion for one with no emigration. So he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as an emigrant. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You shall surely return, Abu Wahb, to the valleys of Makkah. He said: This is a thief who stole a khamisah of mine. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Cut his hand. He said: It is his, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Why not before you brought him to me; but as you have brought him to me, then no. So his hand was cut, and Safwan returned to Makkah."
"18325","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: It was said to Safwan ibn Umayyah: Perished who has no emigration. So he swore not to wash his head until he comes to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he rode his mount and set out, and found the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the door of the mosque. He said: O Messenger of Allah, it was said to me: Perished who has no emigration, so I swore an oath not to wash my head until I come to you. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Safwan heard of Islam and accepted it as religion; and emigration is cut off after the conquest, but jihad and intention remain; and when you are called to march, then march. Then he brought the thief of his khamisah, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered his hand to be cut. He said: I did not intend this, O Messenger of Allah, it is charity upon him. He said: Why not before you brought him to me."
"18326","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah: that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have incurred a hadd, so establish it upon me. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not ask him about it. Prayer was established, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and prayed, with that man with him. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished, the man caught him and said: O Messenger of Allah, I am the man of the hadd, so establish it upon me. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Did you not pray with us just now. He said: Yes. He said: Then go, for you have been forgiven."
"18327","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from al-Shabi, he said: Ibn Masud looked over his house at Kufah, and behold it was crowded with people. He said: Whoever comes to seek fatwa from us, let him sit; we shall give him fatwa if Allah wills. Whoever comes disputing, let him sit until we judge between him and his opponent if Allah wills. Whoever comes wanting to inform us of a private fault Allah has covered for him, let him cover with the cover of Allah, accept the pardon of Allah, and confide his repentance to Him who owns its forgiveness; for we do not own its forgiveness, but we establish its hadd upon him and hold its shame upon him."
"18328","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: that Umar ibn al-Khattab went out one night guarding a caravan halted near Madinah. Until when it was part of the night, he passed a house with people in it, he said: I thought he said: drinking. So he rushed upon them: Indecency. One of them said: Yes, indecency. Allah has forbidden you this. So Umar returned and left them."
"18329","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Musab ibn Zurarah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf: that he guarded one night with Umar ibn al-Khattab. While they were walking, a lamp shone for them in a house, so they headed for it. Until when they drew near it, behold a door ajar upon people with raised voices and clamor in it. Umar said, taking the hand of Abd al-Rahman: Do you know whose house this is. He said: I said: No. He said: It is Rabiah ibn Umayyah ibn Khalaf, and they are now drinking; what do you see. Abd al-Rahman said: I see we have done what Allah forbade us; Allah forbade us and said: And do not spy, and we have spied. So Umar turned away from them and left them."
"18330","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah: that Umar was told that Abu Mihjan al-Thaqafi drinks wine in his house, he and companions of his. So Umar set out until he entered upon him, and behold none with him but a man. Abu Mihjan said: O Commander of the Faithful, this is not lawful for you; Allah has forbidden spying. Umar said: What is this one saying. Zayd ibn Thabit and Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Arqam said to him: He is truthful, O Commander of the Faithful, this is of spying. He said: So Umar went out and left him."
"18331","From Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Amash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, he said: It was said to Ibn Masud: Al-Walid ibn Uqbah is perished; his beard drips wine. He said: We have been forbidden from spying; if it appears to us we establish upon him."
"18332","From Mamar, he said: Budayl al-Uqayli informed me, from Abu al-Rida, he said: A man was raised to Ali and it was said: he stole. He said to him: How did you steal. So he informed him of a matter in which he did not see cutting upon him, so he struck him with lashes and released him."
"18333","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata would say: The hand of the thief is not cut for less than ten dirhams."
"18334","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ata, he said: The hand is cut for ten dirhams."
"18335","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb informed me in the hadith of lost property, he said in it: And the price of the shield is ten dirhams."
"18336","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ibn Masud, he said: The hand was not cut except for a dinar or ten dirhams."
"18337","From al-Muthanna, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the thief steals what reaches the price of the shield, his hand is cut; and the price of the shield was ten dirhams."
"18338","From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Yahya ibn al-Jazzar, from Ali, he said: There is no cutting for less than a dinar or ten dirhams."
"18339","From Yahya ibn Yazid and others, from al-Thawri, from Atiyyah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab was brought a man who stole a garment. He said to Uthman: Value it. So he valued it at eight dirhams, and he did not cut him."
"18340","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Ibn Masud, he said: The hand is not cut except for a shield or a hajafah. He said: I asked Ibrahim: What is its value. He said: A dinar."
"18341","From Mamar, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: The hand of the thief is cut for a dinar or its value."
"18342","From Ibrahim, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The price of the shield for which there is cutting is a dinar. He said: And Dawud ibn al-Husayn informed us, from Ibn al-Musayyib, the like of it."
"18343","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Marwan would narrate that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut the hand of a man for a shield. And the mijann is the shield."
"18344","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah informed me, he said: Urwah informed us: that a thief was not cut in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for less than a shield, a hajafah or a shield, and each of them then was of value; and that the thief was not cut in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for a trivial thing."
"18345","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut the hand of a thief for a shield, and the shield then was of value."
"18346","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Amrah, from Aishah: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The hand of the thief is cut for a quarter dinar and upward."
"18347","From Mamar, from Ata al-Khurasani: that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: When the thief takes what equals a quarter dinar, he is cut."
"18348","From Mamar: that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote: that the hand of the thief be cut for a quarter dinar."
"18349","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Amrah, from Aishah, she said: The hand of the thief is cut for a quarter dinar."
"18350","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, he said: The five are not cut except for five dinars. From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from al-Hasan, the like of the saying of Qatadah."
"18351","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut the hand of a thief for a shield worth three dirhams."
"18352","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut for a shield worth three dirhams. From al-Thawri, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani and Ayyub ibn Musa and Ismail ibn Umayyah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"18353","From al-Thawri, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: Abu Bakr cut for a shield that equals, or what would please me to be mine for, three dirhams. Al-Thawri said: And Shubah informed me, from Qatadah, from Anas, he said: Five dirhams."
"18354","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Said, from Ibn al-Musayyib: that a thief stole an utrunjah worth three dirhams, so Uthman cut his hand. He said: And the utrunjah is a bead of gold on the neck of the boy. From Mamar, from Ayyub, the like of it."
"18355","From al-Thawri, or another, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: that the police of Uthman would steal whips. That reached Uthman, so he said: I swear by Allah, you shall surely leave this, or if a man of you is brought to me who stole the whip of his companion, I shall surely do with him and do."
"18356","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Jafar ibn Muhammad informed me, from his father: that Ali cut for an iron egg."
"18357","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abdullah ibn Abi Mulaykah informed me that two slaves attacked, while he was governor of Taif, the donkey of a woman. She asked them about them, and they said: Hunger drove us to it and we were compelled to it. I said: Were they runaway. He said: I did not know. He said: So I wrote about them to Ibn Abbas, Ubayd ibn Umayr and Abbad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr. Abbad wrote: cut them. Ubayd ibn Umayr wrote: that carrion, blood and pork flesh are made lawful for one compelled. And Ibn Abbas wrote, and I had written to him of their plea of hunger: that you are right; do not cut them, and fine their masters the price of the donkey; and if flogging is therein, then flog them, lest the slave plead hunger."
"18358","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to me, from Urwah: that Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib informed him, from his father, he said: Hatib died leaving slaves, among them those who withhold him, of six thousand, working in the wealth of Hatib, toiling. Umar sent to me one day at noon, while they were with him, and said: These are your slaves; they stole, and there has become due upon them what is due upon the thief, and they slaughtered a she-camel of a man of Muzaynah; they confessed it, and the Muzani is with them. So Umar ordered their hands to be cut. Then he sent after him and turned him back. Then he said to Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib: By Allah, were it not that I think you employ them and starve them, until if one of them found what Allah forbade him he would eat it, I would have cut their hands. But by Allah, since I leave them, I shall surely fine you a fine that pains you. Then he said to the Muzani: What is its price. He said: I would withhold it for four hundred. He said: Give him eight hundred."
"18359","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib: that slave boys of his father Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib stole a camel and slaughtered it. Its skin and head were found with them. Their affair was raised to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he ordered them to be cut. They waited a while, and we thought only that he had finished cutting them. Then Umar said: Bring them to me. Then he said to Abd al-Rahman: By Allah, I see you employ them, then starve them and treat them badly, until if they found what Allah forbade them it would be lawful for them. Then he said to the owner of the camel: How much would you be given for your camel. He said: Four hundred dirhams. He said to Abd al-Rahman: Stand and pay him eight hundred dirhams."
"18360","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi: that Ibn Umar cut the hand of a slave boy of his who stole, and flogged a slave of his who fornicated, without raising them."
"18361","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd Rabbih ibn Abi Umayyah informed me that al-Harith ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Rabiah narrated to him, and Ibn Sabit al-Ahwal, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a slave who had stolen. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, this is a slave who has stolen; his theft was found with him and proof stood upon him. A man said: O Prophet of Allah, this is the slave of so-and-so, orphans; they have no wealth but him. So he left him. Then he was brought a second time, then a third, then a fourth, each time said to him about him as was said the first time. He said: Then he was brought a fifth time and he cut his hand; then a sixth time and he cut his leg; then a seventh time and he cut his hand; then an eighth time and he cut his leg. Then al-Harith said: Four by four: he excused him four times and punished him four times."
"18362","From Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, from Abu al-Zinad, from Abdullah ibn Amir: that Abu Bakr cut the hand of a slave who stole."
"18363","From Ibn Jurayj, from Rabiah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from some of his family: that he attended Abu Bakr cutting the hand of a slave who stole."
"18364","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: I entered upon Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, and he asked me: Is the runaway slave cut when he steals. I said: I have not heard anything about it. Umar said to me: Uthman and Marwan do not cut him. Al-Zuhri said: When Yazid ibn Abd al-Malik succeeded, a runaway slave was raised to him, and he asked me about him. I informed him of what Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz informed me of, from Uthman and Marwan. He said: Have you heard anything about it. I said: No, except what Umar informed me of. He said: By Allah, I shall surely cut him. Al-Zuhri said: So I performed hajj that year, and met Salim ibn Abdullah. He informed me that a slave boy of Abdullah ibn Umar stole while runaway, so Ibn Umar raised him to Said ibn al-As, while he was over Madinah. He said: There is no cutting upon him; you do not cut a runaway. He said: So Ibn Umar took him and cut him, and stood over him until he was cut."
"18365","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ruzayq, the man of Ayla: that he wrote to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz about a runaway who stole. He said: And I used to hear that the runaway is not cut. He said: So Umar wrote to me: that Allah says: As for the male thief and the female thief, cut their hands; if he steals a theft reaching a quarter dinar and just proof stands upon him, then cut him. From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, from Ruzayq, the like of it."
"18366","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, he said: A slave boy of Ibn Umar fled, and passed by slave boys of Aishah. He stole from them a sack with dates in it and rode a donkey of theirs. He was brought to Ibn Umar, so he sent him to Said ibn al-As, while he was governor over Madinah. He said: I heard: a runaway is not cut. He said: Aishah sent to him: My slave boys are but your slave boys; he hungered and rode the donkey to sustain upon it, so do not cut him. So Ibn Umar cut him."
"18367","From al-Thawri and Mamar, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas: that he held there is no cutting upon a runaway slave who steals."
"18368","From Ibrahim, from Salih ibn Kaysan, he said: Ibn al-Zubayr was brought a thieving slave, so he cut his hand."
"18369","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, he said: A man who stole a sheep was brought to Marwan, and behold a strained afflicted man. He said: I do not see he took it but from necessity, so he did not cut him."
"18370","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: Umar said: There is no cutting for a bunch, nor in the year of famine."
"18371","From Mamar, from Aban: that a man came to Umar ibn al-Khattab about a slaughtered she-camel. Umar said to him: Will you take for it two she-camels with it, young, well-fed, fat. He said: For your she-camel; for we do not cut in the year of famine. Murbighatan are the well-nourished."
"18372","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Those of the past would allow the confessions of slaves against themselves, until the judges suspected the slaves that they do that only from hatred of their masters and fleeing from them, so they suspected them in some of what is doubtful."
"18373","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata would say: The confession of the slave against himself is not allowed."
"18374","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, he said: The confession of slaves is not allowed among us, except for hadds."
"18375","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ziyad informed me that he heard Ibn Shihab claiming that Ibn Umar advised Tariq about a slave who confessed against himself. He said: When he brings the sign, meaning: when he corroborates himself, then establish the hadd upon him. Ibn Jurayj said: And Abd al-Karim informed me similar to that."
"18376","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, he said: I asked al-Shabi about a slave who confessed against himself of theft. He said: His confession is not allowed."
"18377","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Isa and Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: The confession of the minor and the owned in wounds is not allowed."
"18378","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: Whatever the slave confesses of a thing established upon him in his body, he is not suspected in his body; and whatever he confesses of a thing that removes him from his masters, his confession is not allowed."
"18379","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: The confession of the slave is not allowed except in theft or adultery."
"18380","From al-Thawri, from Abu Malik al-Ashjai, from elders of theirs: that a slave of Ashja called Abu Jamilah confessed adultery before Ali four times, so he established the hadd upon him."
"18381","Mamar informed us, from a man, from Ikrimah, freedman of Ibn Abbas, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab judged in wounds about which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not judge, nor Abu Bakr. So he judged in the mudihah that is in the body of man and not in his head: that every bone with a named amount, in its mudihah is half a tenth of its amount, whatever it is. When the mudihah is in the hand, then half a tenth of its amount, so long as it is not in the fingers. When it is a mudihah in a finger, in it is half a tenth of the amount of the finger. Whatever is above the fingers in the palm, its amount is like the mudihah of the forearm and upper arm. And in the leg is like what is in the hand. Whatever is of munaqqilah displacing its bones in the forearm or upper arm or leg or thigh, it is half the munaqqilah of the head. And he judged in the fingertips: in each fingertip three she-camels and a third of a she-camel. And he judged in the nail when it is blinded and spoiled: a she-camel. And he judged blood money upon the villagers at twelve thousand dirhams. And he said: I see time differs, and I fear rulers after me for you, that the Muslim man be injured and his blood money go vainly, or his blood money be raised without right and borne upon Muslim peoples and devastate them. There is no increase upon the people of the eyes in aggravation of blood money in the sacred month nor in the sanctity. And the blood money of the villagers is aggravation all of it, with no increase in it over twelve thousand. And he judged in the woman when she is overpowered and her virginity ruptured: a third of her blood money, and no hadd upon her. And he judged in the Magians at eight hundred dirhams. And he said: He is but a slave, not of the People of the Book, so his blood money is like their blood money."
"18382","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged: if the child or the parent dies leaving wealth or wala, it is for his heirs whoever they are. And he judged that the full brother is prior in kalalah in inheritance, then the paternal brother is prior to the sons of the full brother. When the sons of the full and the sons of the paternal are at one level, the sons of the full are prior to the sons of the paternal. When the sons of the paternal are higher by a father than the sons of the full mother and father, the sons of the paternal are prior. When they are equal in lineage, the sons of the father and mother are prior to the sons of the father. And he judged that the full paternal uncle is prior to the paternal uncle, and that the paternal uncle is prior to the sons of the full paternal uncle. When the sons of the father and mother and the sons of the father are at one level, one lineage, the sons of the father and mother are prior to the sons of the father. When they are equal in lineage, the sons of the father and mother are prior to the sons of the father. No paternal uncle nor son of paternal uncle inherits with a brother and son of brother; the brother and son of brother, whoever of them is prior in inheritance, over the paternal uncle and son of paternal uncle. And he judged that whoever has kin of the freed, theirs is his inheritance upon their fixed shares in the Book of Allah; so long as their fixed shares do not exhaust all his wealth, what remains of his inheritance is returned upon them upon their fixed shares, until they inherit all his wealth. And he judged that the disbeliever does not inherit the Muslim, even if he has no heir but him; and that the Muslim does not inherit the disbeliever, so long as he has an heir to inherit him or kinship with him; but if he has no heir to inherit him nor kinship with him, the Muslim inherits him by Islam. And he judged that every wealth divided in the Ignorance is upon the division of the Ignorance, and what Islam reaches while undivided is upon the division of Islam."
"18383","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: I witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judging debt before bequest, while you recite: after a bequest bequeathed or debt. And that the full brothers of the mother inherit to the exclusion of the half brothers; the brothers of the father and mother to the exclusion of the brothers of the mother."
"18384","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Divide wealth among those entitled to fixed shares upon the Book of Allah; whatever the fixed shares leave is for the nearest male man."
"18385","Mamar informed us, from Simak ibn al-Fadl, from Wahb ibn Munabbih, from al-Hakam ibn Masud al-Thaqafi, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab judged in a woman who died leaving her husband, her mother, her maternal brothers and her full brothers. So Umar shared between the maternal brothers and the full brothers in the third. A man said to him: You did not share between them in such-and-such year. Umar said: That was upon what we judged that day, and this is upon what we judge."
"18386","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri: that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: When naught remains but the third between the full brothers and the maternal brothers, they are partners in it, the male like the share of the female."
"18387","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, they said: About the third that belongs to the maternal brothers, they are equal in it, the male and the female. Mamar said: And the people are upon it."
"18388","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father: that he would say about a woman who died leaving her husband, her mother, her maternal brothers and her full sister: For her mother is the sixth, for her husband is the half, and the third is between the maternal brothers and the full sister. And that Umar ibn al-Khattab would say: Cast their father to the wind. As for the full sister, she does not inherit by him; she inherited with the brothers only because she is the daughter of their mother. He said: If with the maternal brothers there is a paternal sister, nothing is for her. I said: How do they divide the third. He said: Ibn Abbas would say: I find naught but the male like the share of two females. Ibn Tawus said: If he has brothers, his mother has the sixth."
"18389","From al-Thawri, from Mansur and al-Amash, from Ibrahim, he said: Umar, Abdullah and Zayd would say: About a woman who left her husband, her mother, her maternal brothers and her full brothers, they said: Their father added to them naught but closeness."
"18390","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali: that he would not give the full brothers with this share anything. From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Mijlaz, he said: Ali would not share them, and Uthman would share them."
"18391","Al-Thawri informed us, from Mabad ibn Khalid, from Masruq: About two daughters and sons of sons, males and females, Masruq said: Aishah would share between them. Then he said: And Ibn Masud would say: For the males to the exclusion of the females; and sisters are in the position of daughters."
"18392","Al-Thawri informed us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, he said: Masruq came from Madinah, so Alqamah said to him: Was any of your companions firmer with you than Abdullah in this. And Abdullah would not share between them. He said: No, but I met Zayd ibn Thabit and the people of Madinah, and they share between them."
"18393","Mamar and al-Thawri informed us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah: that a man died leaving his wife and his parents, in the caliphate of Uthman. So Uthman made it of four shares: he gave his wife a share, his mother a third of the remainder, and his father what remained."
"18394","Al-Thawri informed us, from Mansur and al-Amash, from Ibrahim, he said: Abdullah said: Umar, when he took a path and we followed him in it, we found it easy. He judged in a woman and parents, and made it of four: for his wife the quarter, for the mother a third of what remains, and for the father the surplus. Al-Thawri and Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Muhallab: that Uthman judged like the saying of Umar. From al-Thawri, from Isa, from al-Shabi, from Zayd ibn Thabit the like of that."
"18395","Al-Thawri informed us, from Abu Abdullah, from Fudayl ibn Amr, from Ibrahim, he said: Ibn Abbas opposed the people of prayer in a husband and parents, and made the half for the husband, for the mother the third of the principal wealth, and for the father what remains."
"18396","From al-Thawri, from his father, from al-Musayyib ibn Rafi, from Abdullah, he said: Allah would not see me preferring a mother over a father."
"18397","Al-Thawri informed us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah al-Asbahani, from Ikrimah, he said: Ibn Abbas sent me to Zayd ibn Thabit to ask him about a husband and parents. He said: For the husband is the half, for the mother a third of what remains, and for the father the surplus. Ibn Abbas said: Did you find it in the Book of Allah or an opinion you hold. He said: Rather an opinion I hold; I do not hold to prefer a mother over a father. And Ibn Abbas would give her the third of the whole wealth."
"18398","Mamar informed us, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Zayd ibn Thabit: About a husband and parents: for the husband the half, for the mother a third of what remains, and for the father the surplus."
"18399","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas saying: Allah counted the sands of Alij and did not count this. What is with wealth two thirds and a half. Meaning: that the share is not subjected to awl."
"18400","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: A man came to Ibn Abbas once and said: A man died leaving his daughter and his full sister. Ibn Abbas said: For his daughter is the half, and there is nothing for his sister; what remains is for his male kin. The man said to him: Umar judged otherwise than that; he made for the sister the half and for the daughter the half. Ibn Abbas said: Are you more knowledgeable or Allah. Mamar said: I did not know his saying: Are you more knowledgeable or Allah, until I met Ibn Tawus. I mentioned that to him. Ibn Tawus said: My father informed me that he heard Ibn Abbas saying: Allah Almighty said: If a man perishes having no child but having a sister, hers is half what he leaves. Ibn Abbas said: So you said: hers is the half even if he has a child."
"18401","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, he said: My father informed me that he heard Ibn Abbas saying: Would that I and those who oppose me in the shares gather and place our hands upon the Corner, then supplicate, and make the curse of Allah upon the liars."
"18402","From al-Thawri, from al-Ashath ibn Abi al-Shatha, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid: that Muadh ibn Jabal judged in Yemen about a daughter and a sister, and made for the daughter the half and for the sister the half."
"18403","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: that Muadh judged in Yemen about a daughter and a sister, and made for the daughter the half and for the sister the half."
"18404","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: Ibn Abbas would say: About the sixth which the brothers block for the mother, it is for the brothers. He said: It is not for the father; the mother only takes it to be for the brothers."
"18405","Ibn Tawus said: And it reached me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave them the sixth. He said: So I met some of the children of that man whose brothers were given the sixth, and he said: It reached us that it was a bequest for them."
"18406","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: The father only takes it because he is charged with maintenance upon them, and the mother is not charged with it."
"18407","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: Ibn Abbas would say: The sixth which the mother set aside for the brothers. I said: The maternal brothers. He said: I think them only them. I said: Like them the paternal brothers and the full brothers. He said: Hush. And I had heard from some of our elders from Ibn Abbas that."
"18408","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that Ibn Abbas would say: Inheritance is for the child; then Allah Almighty took from it for the husband and the parent."
"18409","Al-Thawri informed us, from Abu Qays, from Huzayl ibn Shurahbil, from Abdullah ibn Masud: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged in a man who left his daughter, his sons daughter and his sister, and made for the daughter the half, for the sons daughter the sixth, and what remains for the sister. Al-Thawri said: From Abu Qays, from Huzayl, he said: A man came to Abu Musa al-Ashari and Salman ibn Rabiah al-Bahili and asked them about it. They said: For the daughter the half, for the sister the half, and nothing for the sons daughter; and go to Ibn Masud, for he will follow us. He said: The man came to Abdullah and informed him of what they said. He said: I would be astray then and not of the guided; but I shall judge therein with the judgment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he mentioned the like of the first hadith."
"18410","From al-Thawri, he said: Al-Amash and Abu Sahl informed me, from al-Shabi, he said: When there are daughters, sons daughters and a sons son, consider: if sharing is more than the sixth, give them the sixth; and if the sixth is more than sharing, give them the sharing. And another would share them. And it reached us from Ibn Abbas that he would say: Shares we do not subject to awl from six shares. Ata mentioned it from Ibn Abbas. And it reached us from Ali that he was brought about a woman, parents and daughters, and said to the woman: I see your eighth has become a ninth."
"18411","Hisham ibn Hassan informed us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Shurayh: About a husband, a mother, full sisters and maternal brothers, that he made it of ten."
"18412","From al-Thawri, he said: Ibn Abbas would say: Shares are not subjected to awl; the woman, husband, father and mother are subjected to awl. He says: These do not diminish; diminution is only in daughters and sons, and brothers and sisters."
"18413","Al-Thawri informed us, he said: None of the women inherits except six: a daughter, a sons daughter, a mother, a wife, a grandmother and a sister. And the nearest male kin: the son, then the sons son, then the father, then the grandfather, then the brother, then the brothers son, then the paternal uncle, then the uncles son, then the sons of the uncle, the nearest then the nearest. He said: And the grandfather of the grandfather is in the position of the grandfather when there is no father below him, in the position of the sons son."
"18414","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ibn Tawus: One left his father, his mother and his daughter, how. He said: For his daughter the half, not increased; the sixth for the father, the sixth for the mother, then the other sixth for the father. I said: If he leaves his mother and his daughter, for his daughter the half and for his mother the third. He said: Yes; the daughter is not increased over the half. Then he informed me from his father that he said: Attach wealth to the fixed shares; whatever the fixed shares leave of surplus is for the nearest male man. I said: His saying: Attach wealth to the fixed shares, those mentioned in the Quran. He said: Yes."
"18415","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ibn Tawus about a daughter and a sister. He said: My father would mention, from Ibn Abbas, from a man, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about it something, but Tawus was not pleased with that man. He said: My father would withhold about it and say nothing about it, though he was asked about it."
"18416","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abdullah ibn Utbah, who said: al-Dahhak ibn Qays informed me: when there was a plague in Syria and a whole tribe would die so another tribe would inherit them, he wrote about them to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he wrote: if the sons of the father are equal, the sons of the mother have more right, and if the sons of the father are nearer by a father, they have more right than the sons of the father and the mother."
"18417","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from al-Aswad, that Mu'adh ruled in Yemen concerning a daughter and a sister, giving the daughter half and the sister half."
"18418","Al-Thawri reported from Asim, from al-Sha'bi, who said: Umar said: the first grandfather to inherit in Islam."
"18419","Ibn al-Taymi reported from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, who said: I heard al-Sha'bi say: take the matter of the grandfather according to what the people agreed upon."
"18420","Ma'mar and al-Thawri reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abidah al-Salmani, who said: I asked him about an inheritance case in which there was a grandfather, and he said: I memorized from Umar ibn al-Khattab about it one hundred different rulings. He said: I said: from Umar? He said: from Umar, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Abidah, the like of it."
"18421","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Umar said: I have ruled concerning the grandfather with different rulings, and I did not fall short of the truth in them."
"18422","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Umar said: I call you to witness that I have not given a final ruling concerning the grandfather."
"18423","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Nafi', who said: Ibn Umar said: the boldest of you against the embers of Hell is the boldest concerning the grandfather."
"18424","Ma'mar reported: Ayyub informed me from Said ibn Jubayr, from a man of Murad, who said: I heard Ali say: whoever wishes to plunge into the embers of Hell, let him judge between the grandfather and the brothers."
"18425","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: I heard from my father narrating that Ibn al-Zubayr wrote to the people of Iraq that the one to whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If I were to take a close friend until I meet Allah other than Allah, I would have taken Abu Bakr as a close friend, used to make the grandfather a father."
"18426","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri and Qatadah that Abu Bakr made the grandfather a father. Ma'mar said: and Qatadah used to give fatwa by it. Ma'mar said: and I do not know but that al-Zuhri informed me that Uthman used to make the grandfather a father."
"18427","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Hisham ibn Urwah informed me that Urwah narrated to him from Marwan that Umar, when he was stabbed, consulted them about the grandfather, and Uthman said to him: if we follow your view, then your view is right guidance, and if we follow the view of the shaykh before you, then what an excellent man of judgment he was."
"18428","Ma'mar reported from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, that Umar said: I had ruled a ruling concerning the grandfather; if you wish to take it, then do so. And Uthman said: if we follow your view, then your view is right guidance, and if we follow the view of the shaykh before you, then what an excellent man of judgment he was."
"18429","Ibn Uyaynah reported from Amr, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, that he used to consider the grandfather a father, and he recited this verse: The religion of my forefathers, Ibrahim and Ishaq. He said: and Ibn Abbas said: if the jinn knew that there would be a grandfather among mankind, they would not have said: Exalted is the majesty of our Lord."
"18430","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Ata informed me that Ibn Abbas used to make the grandfather a father. Ibn Jurayj said: and Ibn Tawus informed me from his father, from Ibn Abbas, the like of it."
"18431","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, that he used to make the grandfather a father."
"18432","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Ata informed me that Ali used to make the grandfather a father. And one of the people of Iraq rejected that statement of Ata about Ali."
"18433","Al-Thawri reported from Isa, from al-Sha'bi, who said: Umar disliked speaking about the grandfather until he himself became a grandfather, and he said to him: it was my view and the view of Abu Bakr that the grandfather has more right than the brother, and that there is no avoiding discussion about it, so he addressed the people, then asked them whether they had heard anything about it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then a man stood and said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave him the third. He said: who is with him? He said: I do not know. Then he addressed the people again, and a man said: I witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave him the sixth. He said: who is with him? He said: I do not know. Then he asked Zayd ibn Thabit about it, and he struck for him the example of a tree from which branches came out. He said: and he mentioned something I do not remember, and he gave him the third. Al-Thawri said: and it reached me that he said to him: O Commander of the Believers, a tree grew, then a branch split from it, then two branches split from the branch, so what makes the first branch worthier than the second branch, when both branches came out of the first branch? He said: then he asked Ali, and he struck for him the example of a valley in which a flood flowed, and he made him a brother among six, and gave him the sixth. And it reached me from him that when Umar asked Ali, he made for him a flood that flowed and a stream branched from it, then two streams branched, and he said: tell me, if the water of this middle stream dried up, would it return to both streams together? Al-Sha'bi said: Zayd used to make him a brother until they reached three, he being the third of them, and if they exceeded that he gave him the third. And Ali used to make him a brother among six, he being the sixth of them, giving him the sixth, and if they exceeded six he gave him the sixth, and what remained was among them."
"18434","Ma'mar reported from Qatadah, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab called Ali ibn Abi Talib, Zayd ibn Thabit, and Abdullah ibn Abbas, and asked them about the grandfather. Ali said: for him is the third in every case. Zayd said: for him is the third with the brothers, and for him is the sixth of the whole obligation, and he shares as long as sharing is better for him. Ibn Abbas said: he is a father, so the brothers have no inheritance with him. And Allah the Exalted has said: The religion of your father Ibrahim, and between us and him are fathers. He said: so Umar took the saying of Zayd."
"18435","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, who said: these are only the obligations of Umar, but Zayd revived them after him, and they spread from him."
"18436","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab used to share between the grandfather and the brother when there was no one other than them, and he gave him the third with two brothers, and whenever sharing was better for him he shared, and he did not reduce him below the sixth of the whole property. He said: then Zayd revived them after him, and they spread from him."
"18437","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Yahya ibn Said informed me that he read a letter from Muawiyah to Zayd ibn Thabit asking him about the grandfather and the brother, and he wrote to him saying: Allah knows best, and I attended the two caliphs before you, meaning Umar and Uthman, they ruled for the grandfather with one brother half, and with two the third, and when they were more than that, he was not reduced below the third at all."
"18438","Al-Thawri reported from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, who said: Zayd ibn Thabit used to share the grandfather with the brothers and sisters up to the third, and when it reached the third he gave him the third, and the brothers and sisters had what remained, and he counted against the full sister by the paternal brother, then returned upon his brother, and he did not give inheritance to a maternal brother with a grandfather at all, and he counted by the paternal brothers against the paternal and full sisters, and did not give them anything, and when there was a full brother he gave him half, and when there were sisters and a grandfather, he gave him with the sisters the third, and for them two-thirds, and if they were two sisters, he gave them half and for him half."
"18439","Al-Thawri reported from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, who said: Ali used to share the grandfather up to six with the brothers, and he gave every fixed-share holder his share, and he did not give inheritance to a maternal brother with the grandfather, nor a maternal sister, and he did not count by the paternal brother along with the full brother and the grandfather, and he did not increase the grandfather with the child above the sixth, unless there was with him someone else, a brother and a sister, and when there was a full sister and a grandfather and a paternal brother, he gave the sister half, and what remained he gave the grandfather and the brother between them in halves, and if the brothers were many, he shared him with them until the sixth became better for him than sharing, and when the sixth became better for him, he gave him the sixth."
"18440","Al-Thawri reported from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, that Ibn Masud shared the grandfather up to three brothers, and when they were more than that he gave him the third, and if they were sisters he gave them the fixed share, and what remained was for the grandfather, and he did not give inheritance to a maternal brother nor a maternal sister with the grandfather, and he used to say: a paternal brother does not share against a full sister with a grandfather, and he used to say about a full sister and a paternal brother and a grandfather: for the full sister half, and what remains is for the grandfather, and the paternal brother has nothing."
"18441","Ibn Uyaynah reported from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Sha'bi, who said: none of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made the sons of the brother in the place of their father except Ali, and none of the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had more learned companions than Abdullah ibn Masud."
"18442","Al-Thawri said: none used to give inheritance to a brother's son along with his grandfather."
"18443","Al-Thawri reported from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, who said: Umar and Ibn Masud did not prefer a mother over a grandfather."
"18444","A man reported from al-Sha'bi, who said: Ali, Ibn Masud, Zayd ibn Thabit, Uthman, and Ibn Abbas differed about a grandfather, a mother, and a full sister. Ali said: for the sister half, for the mother a third, for the grandfather a sixth. Ibn Masud said: for the sister half, for the mother a sixth, for the grandfather a third. Uthman said: for the mother a third, for the sister a third, for the grandfather a third. Zayd said: it is upon nine shares, for the mother a third, and what remains, two-thirds for the grandfather and a third for the sister. Ibn Abbas said: for the mother a third, and what remains is for the grandfather, and the sister has nothing. Al-Thawri reported from Abd al-Wahid, from Ismail, from Raja, from Ibrahim, the like of it."
"18445","Al-Thawri reported from Abu Ishaq, who said: I came to Shurayh and asked him about a mother, a brother, a grandfather, and a husband. He said: for the husband half, for the mother a third. He said: then he fell silent, so I asked him again, and he said: for the husband half, for the mother a third. He said: then he fell silent, so I asked him again, and he said: for the husband half, for the mother a third. He said: then the one standing over his head said: he does not say anything about the grandfather. He said: so I came to Abidah al-Salmani and he divided it upon six: for the husband half, for the mother one share, for the brother one share, for the grandfather one share. Al-Thawri said: and it reached me that he said: thus Ibn Masud divided it."
"18446","Al-Thawri reported from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Masruq, from Abdullah, that he said about a grandfather, a daughter, and a sister, their obligation is from four: for the daughter two shares, for the grandfather one share, for the sister one share; and if there were two sisters he made it from eight: for the daughter half, four, for the grandfather two shares, for the two sisters one share each; and if they were three sisters he made it from ten shares: for the daughter half, five shares, for the grandfather two shares, for the sisters three shares, one share each."
"18447","Al-Thawri reported from Mansur, from Ibrahim, that Umar ruled about a grandfather, a mother, and a sister, giving the sister half, the mother one share, the grandfather two shares; he did not prefer a mother over a grandfather."
"18448","Al-Thawri reported from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, that Abdullah said: about a mother, a sister, a husband, and a grandfather, it is from eight: for the sister half, three, for the husband half, three, for the mother one share, for the grandfather one share. And Ali said: it is from nine: for the husband three, for the sister three, for the mother two shares, for the grandfather one share. And Zayd said: it is from twenty-seven, and it is al-Akdariyyah, meaning Umm al-Furuj; he made it from nine shares then multiplied it by three, so it became twenty-seven: for the husband nine, for the mother six, for the grandfather eight, for the sister four."
"18449","Al-Thawri reported from al-A'mash, who said: Abdullah said: about a wife, a mother, a brother, and a grandfather, it is from four: for each person among them one share. And other than al-A'mash reported from Ibrahim, from Abdullah, who said: it is from twenty-four: for the mother a sixth, four, for the wife a quarter, six, and what remains between the grandfather and the brother, seven."
"18450","Al-Thawri reported from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, that Abdullah used to say: about a grandfather, a paternal sister, a mother, and two paternal brothers: for the sister half, and what remains is for the grandfather, and the two brothers have nothing."
"18451","Al-Thawri reported from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, who said: none of the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave inheritance to a maternal brother with a grandfather."
"18452","Al-Thawri said: when there is a grandfather and a sister, it is from three: for the grandfather two, for the sister one; and if they are three sisters and a grandfather, it is upon five; and if they are four and a grandfather, it is upon six; and if they are five, multiply three by five, so it is upon fifteen; and when the third is better for the grandfather, multiply the third by a half, then take the third of the whole property and give it to the grandfather, and what remains is according to their shares; and if a mother joins with a sister and a grandfather, it is from nine, for the mother a third, and six remain, for the grandfather four and two for the sister; and if another joins, it is from six, then six is multiplied by four, and that is twenty-four: for the mother a sixth, four, for the grandfather ten, for the two sisters ten; and if they are three sisters and a grandfather, it is from six, a sixth for the mother, and five remain, among them three-fifths for the sisters and two-fifths for the grandfather; and if they are four sisters and a grandfather, sharing and the third become equal, so it is from eighteen: for the mother three, which is the sixth, for the grandfather a third of what remains, five, and ten among the sisters; and whatever more of sisters, it is upon eighteen: the sixth is given to the mother, and a third of what remains to the grandfather; and if it divides evenly, what remains is for the sisters, otherwise the whole is multiplied by the sisters."
"18453","Al-Thawri reported from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: I was told that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fed three grandmothers the sixth. He said: I said to Ibrahim: what are they? He said: his father's two grandmothers, his mother's mother and his father's mother, and his grandmother, his mother's mother."
"18454","Ma'mar reported from Qatadah, who said: when the grandmothers are four, the mother of the mother's father is dropped, and they inherit the sixth in thirds among them."
"18455","Al-Thawri reported from Ash'ath, from al-Sha'bi, who said: four grandmothers came to Masruq, so he gave inheritance to three and dropped the grandmother, the mother of the mother's father."
"18456","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, who said: the grandfather, the mother's father, inherits nothing."
"18457","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Qabisah ibn Dhuwayb, who said: the grandmother came to Abu Bakr seeking her inheritance from her son's son, or her daughter's son, I do not know which of them she was, and Abu Bakr said: I do not find for you in the Book anything, and I did not hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decree for you anything, and I shall ask the people in the evening. When he prayed Zuhr, he turned to the people and said: the grandmother came to me asking me for her inheritance from her son's son, or her daughter's son, and I did not find for her in the Book anything, and I did not hear the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decree for her anything, so did any of you hear from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) anything about her? Then al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah stood and said: I witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decree for her the sixth. He said: did anyone hear that with you? Then Muhammad ibn Maslamah stood and said: I witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decree for her the sixth. So Abu Bakr gave her the sixth. When the caliphate of Umar came, the grandmother who opposed her came to him, and Umar said: the decree was only about other than you, but when you are together, the sixth is between you, and whichever of you is alone with it, it is hers."
"18458","Ibn Uyaynah reported from Yahya ibn Said, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, who said: grandmothers came to Abu Bakr and he gave the inheritance to the mother's mother to the exclusion of the father's mother. Then a man of the Ansar of Banu Harithah called Abd al-Rahman ibn Sahl said to him: O Caliph of the Messenger of Allah, you have given the inheritance to the one whom, if she died, he would not inherit. So he made the inheritance between them."
"18459","Al-Thawri reported from Ibn Dhakwan, from Kharijah ibn Zayd, who said: when the grandmother from the mother's side is the nearer, give her the sixth, and when the grandmother from the mother's side is the nearer, share between them."
"18460","Ibn Uyaynah reported from Abu al-Zinad, who said: I found Kharijah ibn Zayd, Talhah ibn Abdullah ibn Awf, and Sulayman ibn Yasar saying: when the grandmother from the mother's side is nearer, she has more right to it, and when she is farther, they are equal. Ma'mar reported from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that Zayd ibn Thabit used to say that. Al-Thawri reported from Fitr, from a shaykh, from Zayd ibn Thabit, the like of that."
"18461","Ma'mar reported from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from al-Sha'bi, who said: Zayd used to decree for the two grandmothers, whichever of them was nearer, she is first. And Ibn Masud used to equalize between them, whether she was nearer or not."
"18462","Al-Thawri reported from Ash'ath and Abu Sahl, from al-Sha'bi, who said: Ali and Zayd ibn Thabit did not give the grandmother inheritance along with her son, and they gave the near among the grandmothers, from the father's side or from the mother's side. He said: and Abdullah used to give the grandmother inheritance along with her son, and what was near among the grandmothers and what was far, he gave them the sixth when they were from different places, and when they were from one place he gave the near."
"18463","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, that Uthman did not give the grandmother inheritance if her son was alive, and the people were upon it."
"18464","Al-Thawri reported from Mansur and al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, who said: Abdullah said: none veils the grandmothers except the mother."
"18465","Al-Thawri reported from Ash'ath, from Ibn Sirin, who said: the first grandmother the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fed was a father's mother along with her son."
"18466","Ibn Jurayj, al-Thawri, and Ibn Uyaynah reported from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, who said: I heard Said ibn al-Musayyib say: Umar ibn al-Khattab gave a grandmother inheritance along with her son. Ibn Jurayj and Ibn Uyaynah said: a woman from Thaqif, one of Banu Nadlah."
"18467","Hisham ibn Hassan reported from Anas ibn Sirin, that Shurayh used to give the grandmother inheritance along with her son while he was alive."
"18468","Ibn Uyaynah reported from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu al-Sha'tha, who said: the grandmother inherits along with her son."
"18469","Ma'mar reported from Bilal ibn Abi Burdah, that Abu Musa al-Ash'ari used to give the grandmother inheritance along with her son, and Bilal ruled by that while he was amir over Basra."
"18470","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ibn Shihab, who said: the first who gave the two grandmothers inheritance was Umar ibn al-Khattab, so he combined between them."
"18471","Ma'mar reported from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, who said: Zayd ibn Thabit did not give the grandmother, the father's mother, inheritance while her son was alive."
"18472","Ma'mar reported from a man of the children of Abu Burdah, from Abu Burdah, that Abu Musa al-Ash'ari gave her inheritance while her son was alive, and Bilal ruled by that in his governorship over Basra."
"18473","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, that he gave her inheritance along with her son."
"18474","Al-Thawri reported from Mansur and al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, who said: Abdullah said: the enslaved brothers and the Christians veil the mother but do not inherit. Al-Thawri said in this hadith, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim: only the wife, the husband, and the mother veil, and none other veils."
"18475","Al-Thawri reported from Abu Sahl, from al-Sha'bi, that Ali and Zayd said: they neither veil nor inherit. Al-Thawri said: and the killer with us is at that station, he neither veils nor inherits."
"18476","Al-Thawri reported from a man, from Ibn Sirin, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, who said: none who does not inherit veils."
"18477","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Ibn Urwah informed me from his father that he asked him about a man who died leaving an owned slave girl and his grandmother, his mother's mother, free: does she inherit him? He said: yes, she inherits him."
"18478","Ibn Uyaynah reported from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Abu Amr al-Shaybani, that a freedman of a people died leaving only his brother's son, while his brother was enslaved, and Shurayh had ruled with the inheritance for the patrons, and it was said to his brother: do you have a child? He said: yes, a free son. So he came to Shurayh and he returned the inheritance to him."
"18479","Ma'mar reported from Qatadah, who said: the killer neither veils nor inherits. He said: and the slave, the Jew, and the Christian are at that station."
"18480","Al-Thawri reported from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Abu Sadiq, from Ali, who said: none who does not inherit veils."
"18481","Ma'mar reported from Zayd ibn Aslam, who said: a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, a man died leaving his maternal aunt and his paternal aunt. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: the maternal aunt and the paternal aunt, repeating them both, thus awaiting revelation about them, and nothing came to him about them. Then the man returned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after that, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned with the like of his saying three times, and nothing came to him about them. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: nothing has come to me about them."
"18482","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, who said: the paternal aunt and the maternal aunt inherit nothing."
"18483","Ibrahim ibn Abi Yahya reported from Safwan ibn Sulaym, that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, a man left his maternal aunt and his paternal aunt, and nothing was revealed to him about that, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: they have nothing."
"18484","Al-Thawri reported from al-Hasan ibn Amr al-Fuqaymi, from Ghalib ibn Abbad, from Qays ibn Habtar al-Nahshali, who said: Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan wrote asking about a paternal aunt and a maternal aunt, and a shaykh said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab gave the paternal aunt two-thirds and the maternal aunt a third. So Abd al-Malik was about to write with it, then he said: so where is Zayd ibn Thabit?"
"18485","Al-Thawri reported from Yunus, from al-Hasan, that Umar ruled about a paternal aunt and a maternal aunt, giving the paternal aunt two-thirds and the maternal aunt a third."
"18486","Ma'mar reported from Hafs ibn Sulayman and others, from al-Hasan, that Umar ibn al-Khattab gave the paternal aunt and the maternal aunt inheritance, giving the paternal aunt two-thirds and the maternal aunt a third."
"18487","Al-Thawri reported from Muhammad ibn Salim, from al-Sha'bi, from Ibn Masud, who said: the paternal aunt is at the station of the father, the maternal aunt is at the station of the mother, the brother's daughter is at the station of the brother, and every possessor of kinship takes the station of his kin by which he inherits, when there is no inheriting near heir."
"18488","Al-Thawri reported from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from al-Sha'bi, from Masruq, who said: bring them down to the station of their fathers."
"18489","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq said about a man who left his paternal aunt and his maternal aunt: for his paternal aunt two-thirds of his property and for his maternal aunt a third. I said to Abd al-Karim: and a mother with them? He said: they think, and I, that the mother has more right. I said to Abd al-Karim: and his daughter with the maternal aunt and the paternal aunt? He said: they think, and I, that the daughter has the whole property to the exclusion of them. I said to Abd al-Karim: and a daughter of a daughter of a paternal aunt, and a maternal aunt? He said: for the daughter of the daughter of the paternal aunt two-thirds, and for the maternal aunt a third. He said: and they say from Ibn Masud that he ruled about a mother and a maternal brother: for his brother a sixth, and what remains is for his mother."
"18490","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, about a man who left his daughter, his paternal aunt, and his maternal aunt, who said: for his daughter the whole property."
"18491","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: when a man leaves his maternal sister, and this sort along with the maternal aunt and the paternal aunt, the whole property is for his maternal sister."
"18492","Al-Thawri reported from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from his uncle Wasi' ibn Habban, who said: Thabit ibn al-Dahdahah died, and he was a man who had settled among Banu Unayf, or among Banu al-Ajlan, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: does he have an heir? And they found no heir for him. He said: so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave his inheritance to his sister's son, Abu Lubabah ibn Abd al-Mundhir."
"18493","Ibrahim ibn Abi Yahya reported from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, who said: Ibn al-Dahdahah died and left no heir other than his sister's son, Abu Lubabah ibn Abd al-Mundhir, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave him his inheritance."
"18494","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, who said: I heard in Madinah that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah and His Messenger are the patron of one who has no patron, and the maternal uncle is the heir of one who has no heir. Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Ibn Tawus informed me from a truthful man, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of the hadith of Ma'mar."
"18495","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Amr ibn Muslim informed me, saying: Tawus narrated from Aishah that she said: Allah and His Messenger are the patron of one who has no patron, and the maternal uncle is the heir of one who has no heir."
"18496","Al-Thawri reported from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from al-Sha'bi, who said about a brother's daughter and a paternal aunt: the property is for the brother's daughter, and the paternal aunt has nothing. And another said: the property is between them in halves."
"18497","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: when a man dies leaving his daughter, his maternal brothers, his maternal uncles, his paternal aunt, and this sort, the whole property is for his daughter."
"18498","Al-Thawri reported from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: it used to be said: the possessor of a share has more right than one who has no share."
"18499","Al-Thawri reported from Muhammad ibn Salim, from al-Sha'bi, and Mansur said it, both said: Ali used to return upon every possessor of a share according to his share, except the husband and the wife. And Abdullah: he did not return upon a maternal sister along with a mother, nor upon a son's daughter along with an own daughter, nor upon a paternal sister along with a full sister, nor upon a grandmother, nor upon a wife, nor upon a husband."
"18500","Al-Thawri reported from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: it used to be said: the possessor of a share has more right than one who has no share."
"18501","Hushaym reported from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani, from al-Sha'bi, who said: it was said to him: Abu Ubaydah gave a sister the whole property. So al-Sha'bi said: one who is better than Abu Ubaydah did that; Abdullah ibn Masud used to do that."
"18502","Hushaym reported from Mughirah, from al-Sha'bi, who said: Zayd ibn Thabit never returned anything upon the possessors of kinship at all."
"18503","Al-Thawri reported from Muhammad ibn Salim, from al-Sha'bi, from Kharijah ibn Zayd, from Zayd, that he used to give the possessors of obligations their obligations and put what remained in the public treasury."
"18504","Al-Thawri reported from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, who said: it was mentioned to Ali about a man who left his cousins, one of them his maternal brother, that Ibn Masud gave him the whole property, and he said: may Allah have mercy on Abdullah, he was indeed learned; had I been, I would have given him his share then shared between them. Al-Thawri reported from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, that he used to say in it the saying of Abdullah."
"18505","Al-Thawri reported from al-A'mash, from Abu Wail, who said: the letter of Umar ibn al-Khattab came to us: when the agnates, one of them nearer by a mother, then give him the property."
"18506","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abdullah ibn Utbah, who said: al-Dahhak ibn Qays informed me that there was a plague in Syria and a tribe would die entirely so another tribe would inherit it, and he wrote about them to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and Umar wrote: when the sons of the father are equal, the nearest of them by the mother is first, and when the sons of the father are nearer, they have more right than the sons of the father and the mother."
"18507","Ma'mar reported from Qatadah, who said: Hisham ibn Hubayrah, a judge of the people of Basra, wrote to Shurayh asking him about a man who divorced his wife while sick, about a man who acknowledged his child at death, and about a woman who died leaving her two cousins, one of them her husband and the other her maternal brother. So Shurayh wrote to him: about the one he divorced while sick, that she inherits him as long as she is in the waiting period; and he wrote to him about the one who acknowledged his child at death, that he is attached to him; and he wrote to him about the one who died leaving her two cousins, one of them her husband and the other her maternal brother: for her husband half, for her maternal brother a sixth, and what remains is between them."
"18508","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Amr ibn Shuayb said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ruled that every claimed child claimed after his father, claimed by his heir, he ruled that if he was from a slave girl he owned at the time he had intercourse with her, then he is attached to the one who claimed him, but he has nothing of the inheritance of his father to whom he is claimed, unless the one who claimed him gives him inheritance from his share, and that whatever inheritance they inherited after he was claimed, he has his share of it, and he ruled that if he was from a slave girl his alleged father did not own, or from a free woman he committed adultery with, he ruled that he is not attached and does not inherit, and if the one to whom he is claimed is the one who claimed him, then he is a child of adultery belonging to his mother's people, whoever they were, free or slave."
"18509","And he said: the child belongs to the bed, and for the adulterer is the stone."
"18510","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Muhammad ibn Abi Layla said to me: if a man dies and he had a slave girl who has a child, and two just men among the heirs testify that their father attached him and acknowledged him, then he is an heir with them; and if they are two men, sons of the deceased, one testified that his father claimed him and the other denied, and they differ about it, we say: for the one who denied is half the inheritance, for the one who acknowledged and testified a third of the inheritance, and for the one claimed a sixth of the inheritance, his sixth in the half of the one who acknowledged and testified, and his other sixth in the half of the one who denied and did not acknowledge nor testify by him. I said: and thus they say about the one whom some of the heirs acknowledge, and they rule by the share of what they inherited? He said: yes. I said: if two men inherited one hundred dinars, and one testified that his companion owes ten dinars, and the other denied, the one who testified rules five. Muhammad said: we do not raise any of this to the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), but to our jurists below that. Ibn Jurayj said: and I say: if one of the heirs testifies to a right of a people, and the others deny, then the oath of the claimant along with his testimony."
"18511","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: one of our companions informed me that Tawus ruled in al-Jund about sons of a father, one testified that his father claimed a slave who was among them, and Tawus did not permit his claiming him, nor attach him by lineage, but he gave the slave a fifth of the inheritance in the property of the one who testified that his father claimed him, and freed what remained of the slave in the property of the one who testified."
"18512","Al-Thawri said about the heir who acknowledges a debt upon the dead: Hammad said: what is in the hands of the acknowledger is taken in full, because the heir has nothing until the debt is settled. Hammad said: and when two of the heirs testify to lineage, they have no testimony because they repel from themselves, but it is taken from their share. Al-Thawri reported from Mughirah, from al-Sha'bi, that he said: by the shares. And Ibn Abi Layla said it."
"18513","Al-Thawri reported from Mansur or another, from Ibrahim, who said: when two of the heirs testify, it is effective upon them in the whole property. Al-Thawri said: and al-Ashath ibn Sawwar informed me from al-Hasan the like of that. He said: and al-Qasim ibn al-Walid informed me from al-Harith, from Ibrahim, the like of it, from Abdullah, from Shubah, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, the like of that. Shubah said: and al-Hakam informed me from Ibrahim, who said: when two of the heirs testify about the debt, it is effective in their share, the like of the saying of Hammad."
"18514","Al-Thawri said about three brothers, one acknowledged a brother of his and the other two denied, leaving three thousand dirhams: Hammad used to say: half of a thousand falls upon the one who acknowledged him. He said: and another said: it is effective upon him in his share, so upon him in his share is a quarter, a quarter of a thousand, and everything the claimed one inherits in future of kinship or patronage, the claimed one shares with him in it upon this reckoning, and he is not attached by lineage, nor do they inherit each other, and whoever denies the claimed one is not flogged for him, and if the one who claimed him denies him he is not flogged, and if two testify, the inheritance is secured and he is attached by lineage, and the one who claimed him may not disavow him in inheritance when two of the heirs or others testify."
"18515","Al-Thawri said: when a man acknowledges to a man that he is his brother, and acknowledges to him a debt, he has the lesser of them when he has no proof, and when the one who claimed him dies, what was between them is cut off."
"18516","Ma'mar said about a man who says at death: the son of this slave girl of mine is my son, and some of his children testify by that: we heard that his inheritance is in the share of the one who testified by him. He said: and if only one testified, he inherits in his share the like of his share, or is attached with them, but he does not inherit his father nor is claimed for him until two testify."
"18517","Al-Thawri said: had a woman come with a boy and said: this is my son from a man I married, she is not believed in that unless she brings proof, because she intended to remove people from their inheritance, and there is no inheritance between them and that boy, while a man when he comes with a boy and claims him, he inherits him and he is attached to him; the man is not like the woman. He said: and had a man disavowed a son of his, then the grandfather claimed him after, saying: he is my son's son, he is not attached by his lineage, nor is the testimony of the grandfather for him permitted, nor do the grandfather and the boy inherit each other except in the property the boy's father left."
"18518","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: a woman freed a boy, or a person, and a man took him to himself and spent upon him, and the woman said to her son: dispute with him to Shurayh. He said: my mother freed this one, and this one took him to himself and seized him. The man said: I found a lost person and took him to myself and spent upon him. So Shurayh said: he is with the one who benefits him."
"18519","Ma'mar reported from Jabir ibn Yazid al-Jufi, from al-Sha'bi, that Umar and Ali ruled about people who die together, it not being known which of them died first, that some of them inherit some."
"18520","Al-Thawri reported from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, that Umar gave some of them inheritance from some from their original properties; he did not give them inheritance from what some of them inherit from some at all."
"18521","Al-Thawri reported from Huraysh, from his father, from Ali, that two brothers were killed at Siffin, or a man and his son, and he gave one of them inheritance from the other."
"18522","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ibn Abi Layla, that Umar and Ali said about people drowned together, it not being known which of them died first, as if they were three brothers who died together, each man among them having a thousand dirhams and their mother alive: this one inherits his mother and his brother, and this one inherits his mother and his brother, so for the mother from each man among them is a sixth of what he left, and for the brothers what remains, all of them likewise, then the mother returns and inherits besides the sixth she inherited first time from each man, of what he inherited from his brother, a third."
"18523","Ibn Jurayj reported, and Humayd al-Araj said: the inheritance of this is taken and put in the property of this, and the inheritance of this is taken and put in the inheritance of this."
"18524","Al-Thawri reported from al-A'mash, Mansur, and Mughirah, from Ibrahim, that he gave the drowned some inheritance from some."
"18525","Al-Thawri reported from Abu Sahl, that he asked Ibrahim about three brothers who drowned or died together, having a living mother, and he gave her from each one a sixth, then gave some of them inheritance from some, then gave her after a third from each one of what he inherited from his fellow."
"18526","Al-Thawri reported from Mughirah, from al-Haytham ibn Qatan, who said: my wife and my daughter died together, they drowned or something befell them, and Shurayh gave some of them inheritance from some."
"18527","Al-Thawri reported from Abu al-Za'ra, from Abdullah ibn Utbah ibn Masud, that he gave some of them inheritance from some."
"18528","Al-Thawri and Ibn Uyaynah reported from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu al-Minhal, from Iyas ibn Abd, who was of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that a house fell upon people, and he gave some of them inheritance from some."
"18529","Abbad ibn Kathir reported from Abu al-Zinad, from Kharijah ibn Zayd, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that he used to give the living inheritance from the dead, but did not give the dead inheritance some from some."
"18530","Al-Thawri and Ma'mar reported from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, that he gave the living inheritance from the dead, and did not give the dead inheritance some from some. Ma'mar said: he wrote with that. He said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz ruled with the like of that."
"18531","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, who said: the sunnah went that every dead one's living heir inherits him, and the dead do not inherit some from some. Ibn Jurayj reported from al-Zuhri the like of it."
"18532","Ibn Jurayj reported from Yahya ibn Said, that the people of al-Harrah and the companions of the Camel did not inherit one another."
"18533","Abbad ibn Kathir reported from Abu al-Zinad, from Kharijah ibn Zayd, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that he gave the living inheritance from the dead, and did not give the dead inheritance some from some, and that was on the day of al-Harrah."
"18534","Abd al-Razzaq said: and he informed us also from Abu al-Zinad, from Kharijah ibn Zayd, that Abu Bakr ruled about the people of al-Yamamah the like of the saying of Zayd ibn Thabit: he gave the living inheritance from the dead, and did not give the dead inheritance some from some."
"18535","Abu Muti' reported, saying: Abbad ibn Kathir was taken out of his grave after three years, nothing missing from him except hairs. He said: so we knew that this shows us his merit, and he was with us trustworthy."
"18536","Al-Thawri reported from Abu Sahl, from al-Sha'bi, that Ali and Ibn Masud used to give the Magians inheritance from two places."
"18537","Al-Thawri reported from a man, from Ibrahim, that he used to give them inheritance from two places."
"18538","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, who said: he gives them inheritance by the nearest kin to him."
"18539","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata and others, who said: they do not inherit one another until lineage is witnessed."
"18540","Al-Thawri reported from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh, that Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to him: the carried child is not given inheritance except with proof. Ma'mar reported from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh, the like of it. Al-Thawri reported from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh, the like of it. Al-Thawri said: and we are upon this, we do not give him inheritance except with proof."
"18541","Ma'mar reported from Asim ibn Sulayman, who said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote: the two carried ones do not inherit each other in pre-Islamic birth."
"18542","Ma'mar reported, saying: Asim informed me that al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin faulted that upon him, saying: what is with them that they do not inherit one another when they are known and proof is established?"
"18543","Ibrahim ibn Abi Yahya reported from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, who said: Uthman did not give inheritance by the birth of the non-Arabs when they were born outside Islam."
"18544","Israel reported from Ashath ibn Abi al-Sha'tha, who said: I disputed to Shurayh about a freedwoman of the tribe who died leaving much property, and a man came and disputed her patrons, bringing proof that she used to say: my brother, so Shurayh gave him the whole property."
"18545","Al-Thawri reported from Hammad, from Ibrahim, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: every lineage connected upon in Islam, it is inheriting and inherited."
"18546","Ma'mar reported from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, that Uthman did not give inheritance by the birth of the people of polytheism."
"18547","Ma'mar reported from one who heard Ibrahim say: when they connect in Islam, some of them inherit from some."
"18548","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote a writing about the grandfather and the kalalah, and remained seeking Allah's choice, saying: O Allah, if You know in it good, then carry it out, until when he was stabbed, he called for the writing and erased it, and none knew what was in it, and he said: I wrote a writing about the grandfather and the kalalah, and I was seeking Allah's choice in it, and I saw to leave you upon what you were upon."
"18549","Al-Thawri reported from Amr ibn Murrah, from Umar, who said: three, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had explained them to us, are dearer to me than the world and what is in it: the caliphate, the kalalah, and usury."
"18550","Ibn Jurayj and Ibn Uyaynah reported from Amr ibn Dinar, from Muhammad ibn Talhah ibn Yazid ibn Rukanah, who said: Umar said: that I had asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about three is dearer to me than red camels: about the kalalah, about the caliph after him, and about people who said: we affirm zakah in our properties but we do not pay it to you, is fighting them lawful or not? He said: and Abu Bakr saw fighting."
"18551","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, who said: Umar said to me when he was stabbed: retain from me three: leadership is consultation, and in ransom of the Arabs, in place of every slave a slave, and in the son of the slave girl two slaves, and in the kalalah what I said. He said: I said to Ibn Tawus: what did he say? And he refused to inform me."
"18552","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Ibn Tawus informed me from his father, from Ibn Abbas, that Umar ibn al-Khattab enjoined at death, saying: the kalalah is as I said. Ibn Abbas said: and what did you say? He said: one who has no child."
"18553","Ibn Uyaynah reported from Sulayman al-Ahwal, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, who said: I am the latest of them in covenant with Umar, and he said: the kalalah is what I said. He said: and what did you say? He said: one who has no child. I thought he said: nor parent."
"18554","Ibn Jurayj and Ibn Uyaynah reported from Amr ibn Dinar, from Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ali, who said: I heard Ibn Abbas say: the kalalah is one who has no child and no parent. Ibn Uyaynah added, saying: Hasan ibn Muhammad said: I said to Ibn Abbas: but Allah says: if a man perishes having no child. He said: so he rebuked me."
"18555","Al-Thawri reported from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, from Abu Bakr, that he said: the kalalah is what is besides the child and the parent."
"18556","Ibn Uyaynah reported from Asim ibn Sulayman, from al-Sha'bi, who said: Abu Bakr used to say: the kalalah is one who has no child and no parent. He said: and Umar used to say: the kalalah is one who has no child. Then when Umar was stabbed, he said: I am ashamed before Allah to oppose Abu Bakr; I see the kalalah is what is besides the child and the parent."
"18557","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, Qatadah, and Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, who said: the kalalah is one who has neither child nor parent."
"18558","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: there was revealed: Allah gives you fatwa about the kalalah, while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was on a journey of his, and beside him Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) conveyed it to Hudhayfah, and Hudhayfah conveyed it to Umar ibn al-Khattab while he was walking behind Hudhayfah. When Umar was made caliph, he asked Hudhayfah about it, hoping he had its explanation, and Hudhayfah said to him: by Allah, you are indeed foolish if you thought your rule would carry me to narrate to you about it what I did not narrate to you that day. Umar said: I did not mean this, may Allah have mercy on you. Ma'mar said: so Ayyub informed me from Ibn Sirin that Umar, when he read: Allah makes clear to you lest you go astray, said: O Allah, whomever You made the kalalah clear to, You did not make it clear to me."
"18559","Ibn Uyaynah reported from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, that Umar ordered Hafsah to ask the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the kalalah, and she gave him respite until when he wore his clothes she asked him, and he dictated it to her on a shoulder blade. Umar said: he ordered you with this; I do not think he will understand it, or did the summer verse not suffice him? She came with it to Umar and he read it, and when he read: Allah makes clear to you lest you go astray, he said: O Allah, whomever You made clear to, You did not make clear to me."
"18560","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that Umar ordered Hafsah to ask the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the kalalah."
"18561","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed that Ibn Abbas said: When Abu Bakr died, an ally of his took a sixth of his property. Ibn Abbas said to him: And he was commanded to do that? He said: So I asked about that myself, and I found no one who knew of that."
"18562","From Mamar, from Qatadah, regarding His saying: 'And for all We made mawali' (4:33): he said: They are the allies and protectors. And regarding: 'and those with whom your oaths bound a covenant': he said: In Jahiliyyah a man would covenant with a man, saying: My blood is your blood, my ruin is your ruin; you inherit from me and I inherit from you, you seek revenge for my blood and I seek revenge for yours. When Islam came, some of them remained, and they were commanded to give them their share of inheritance, which is a sixth. Then that was abrogated afterwards by the inheritance laws. And he said: 'And blood relatives are more entitled to one another.'"
"18563","Al-Thawri informed us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, regarding His saying: 'And for all We made mawali': he said: They are the allies. And regarding: 'and those with whom your oaths bound': he said: This was an alliance in Jahiliyyah; when Islam came they were commanded to give them their share of help, wala (allegiance), and counsel, but no inheritance."
"18564","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no alliance in Islam, but hold fast to the alliance of Jahiliyyah."
"18565","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed that whoever was an ally in Jahiliyyah remains upon his alliance, and he has his share of the blood-money and support; those he allied with pay blood-money on his behalf, and his inheritance belongs to his paternal relatives, whoever they are. And they said: There is no alliance in Islam, but hold fast to the alliance of Jahiliyyah, for Allah has only increased it in Islam in strictness. Amr said: And Umar ibn al-Khattab decreed that whoever was an ally or a counted member among a people who paid blood-money on his behalf and supported him, his inheritance belongs to them when no known heir exists."
"18566","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab decreed that whoever among the Muslims dies with no known heir, and he was not with a people with whom he shares blood-money and counted numbers, his inheritance is among the Muslims, in the wealth of Allah that is distributed among them."
"18567","From al-Thawri, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Shabi, from Shurayh, that he said: The captive in the hands of the enemy is inherited from. And Ibrahim said the same."
"18568","From al-Thawri, he said: When the apostate is killed, his property belongs to his heirs; but if he joins the land of war, his property belongs to the Muslims."
"18569","Al-Thawri informed us, from Mughirah, from al-Shabi, from Ali: that he gave a hermaphrodite male inheritance as a male, according to where he urinates from."
"18570","Mamar informed us, from Qatadah, he said: I asked Said ibn al-Musayyib about one created with the form of a woman and the form of a man, how is he given inheritance? He said: He is given inheritance according to whichever of the two he urinates from. Qatadah said: Then Ibn al-Musayyib said: What if he urinates from both together? I said: I do not know. He said: Look from which of the two the urine comes out faster, and inheritance is given on that basis. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Said ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, the same."
"18571","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I was told that Amir ibn al-Zarib al-Urwani, who used to judge among the people in Jahiliyyah, had a dispute over a male hermaphrodite brought to him, and he did not know, until his slave-girl, a shepherdess of his sheep, advised him: Look, from wherever he urinates, give him inheritance accordingly."
"18572","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told that a similar case was disputed before Laqit ibn Zurarah, and he did not know, until Khusaylah, his slave-girl, a shepherdess of his sheep, advised him to assign him according to where he urinates from."
"18573","Ibn Jurayj said: I was told from Zayd ibn Aslam, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not ask the People of the Book about anything, for if they guide you they have already misguided themselves. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, shall we not narrate from the Children of Israel? He said: Narrate, and there is no harm."
"18574","Al-Awzai informed us, from Hassan ibn Atiyyah, from Abu Kabshah, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Convey from me even a verse, and narrate from the Children of Israel and there is no harm; whoever lies upon me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire."
"18575","Al-Thawri informed us, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, from Ata ibn Yasar, he said: The Jews used to narrate to the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they would listen eagerly as though amazed. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not believe them and do not deny them, but say: We believe in what was sent down to us and sent down to you, and our God and your God is One, and we submit to Him."
"18576","Al-Thawri informed us, from Umarah, from Hurayth ibn Zuhayr, he said: Abdullah said: Do not ask the People of the Book about anything, for they will never guide you when they have misguided themselves; lest you deny a truth or affirm a falsehood. And there is none of the People of the Book but in his heart is a recitation calling him to Allah and His Book. Man added, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abdullah, in this hadith, that he said: If you must ask them, then look at what agrees with the Book of Allah and take it, and what contradicts the Book of Allah, leave it."
"18577","Al-Thawri informed us, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, and from Abdullah ibn Thabit; and he said: from al-Shabi, from Abdullah ibn Thabit, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab came and said: O Messenger of Allah, I passed by a brother of mine from the Jews, and he wrote for me compendia from the Torah; shall I not present them to you? The face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) changed. Abdullah said: May Allah confound your mind, do you not see the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? Umar said: I am pleased with Allah as Lord, with Islam as religion, and with Muhammad as Messenger. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was relieved, then said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, if Musa were alive among you and you followed him and left me, you would go astray. You are my portion from the nations, and I am your portion from the prophets."
"18578","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Ibn Abi Namlah al-Ansari informed us, that Abu Namlah informed him that while he was sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a Jewish man came, and a funeral bier passed by. He said: O Muhammad, does it (the dead) speak? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah knows best. The Jew said: It does speak. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whatever the People of the Book narrate to you, do not believe them and do not deny them, but say: We believe in Allah and His Books and His Messengers; if it is false you will not have believed it, and if it is true you will not have denied it."
"18579","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, that Ibn Abbas said: How can you ask them about anything when the Book of Allah is among you?"
"18580","Al-Thawri informed us, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from Awf ibn Malik al-Ashjai: that a Jewish or Christian man thrust at a Muslim woman, then threw dust over her, seeking her for himself. That was reported to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he said: These people have a covenant only so long as they fulfill your covenant to you; when they do not fulfill a covenant to you, they have no covenant. Then Umar crucified him."
"18581","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ata said: If there is a close kinship between a Muslim and a disbeliever, let him visit him when sick. And Amr ibn Dinar said the same. Ata said: But if there is no kinship between them, let him not visit him. And Amr said: Let him visit him even if there is no kinship between them, as an opinion."
"18582","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I heard Sulayman ibn Musa saying: We visit them even if there is no kinship between us and them."
"18583","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abdullah informed me; Abd al-Razzaq said: And I heard it myself from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Alqamah, from Ibn Abi Husayn, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had a Jewish neighbor of good character. He fell ill, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited him with his Companions and said: Do you bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah? He looked at his father; his father was silent and the boy was silent. Then a second time, then a third; on the third his father said: Say what he told you. So he did, then died. The Jews wanted to take charge of him, but the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We have more right to him than you. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) washed him, shrouded him, embalmed him, and prayed over him."
"18584","Ibn al-Taymi informed us, from his father, he said: Qatadah informed me, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a Christian man: Accept Islam, Abu al-Harith! The Christian said: I have already accepted Islam. He said to him: Accept Islam, Abu al-Harith! He said: I have already accepted Islam. He said to him a third time: Accept Islam, Abu al-Harith! He said: I accepted Islam before you. He became angry and said: You lie; three traits have barred you from Islam: your partaking of wine, and he did not say your drinking, and your eating pork, and your claiming a son for Allah."
"18585","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Ibn Abi Najih, he said: I heard Mujahid saying to a Christian boy of his: O Jarir, accept Islam! Then he said: That is how it used to be said to them."
"18586","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym informed me, that Muhammad ibn al-Aswad ibn Khalaf informed him that his father al-Aswad saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pledging allegiance to the people on the day of the Conquest. He said: He sat at Qarn Masqalah, and Qarn Masqalah is where the houses of Ibn Abi Yamamah drain, which is the house of Ibn Samurah and around it; what drains from its rear goes toward the house of Ibn Amir, and what faces forward goes toward the house of Ibn Samurah and around it. Al-Aswad said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sitting, and the people, old and young, came to him and pledged to him upon Islam, and the testimony of faith in Allah, and the testimony that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger."
"18587","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abbas ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Mina informed me, that two men of Muzaynah were evil men who had cut off roads and killed. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by them, so they performed ablution and prayed, then pledged allegiance to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and said: O Messenger of Allah, we intended to come to you, but Allah shortened our steps. He said: What are your names? They said: The two humiliated ones. He said: Rather, you are the two honored ones."
"18588","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I was informed from Uthaym ibn Kulayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I have accepted Islam. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Cast off from you the hair of disbelief, meaning: shave."
"18589","Ibn Jurayj said: And another informed me from him that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to another: Cast off from you the hair of disbelief and get circumcised."
"18590","Al-Thawri informed us, from al-Agharr, from Khalifah ibn Husayn, from his grandfather Qays ibn Asim, he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intending Islam, and I accepted Islam. He commanded me to wash with water and lote leaves, so I washed with water and lote leaves."
"18591","Ubaydullah and Abdullah, the sons of Umar, informed us, from Said ibn Abi Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, that Thumamah al-Hanafi was taken captive and accepted Islam. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to him and sent him to the garden of Abu Talhah and ordered him to wash. So he washed and prayed two rakas, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your brother's Islam has become good."
"18592","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: I heard him saying: Regarding one who accepts Islam, he is ordered to wash."
"18593","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I was told a hadith traced back to Ali, regarding a Jew or Christian who becomes a heretic (zindiq): he said: Leave him; he has moved from one religion to another religion."
"18594","I heard Abu Hanifah, he said: There was brought before Ali a Jew or Christian who had become a heretic. He said: Leave him; he has moved from disbelief to disbelief. Abd al-Razzaq said: I said to him: From whom is this? He said: From Simak ibn Harb, from Qabus ibn al-Mukhariq, that Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr wrote to Ali about him, and Ali wrote back to him with this."
"18595","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Khallad informed me, that Amr ibn Shuayb informed him, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: We do not leave a Jew or Christian to make his child Christian or Jewish in the dominion of the Arabs."
"18596","Mamar informed us, from one who heard al-Hasan, saying: It is of the Sunnah that the churches in the old and new cities be demolished."
"18597","Mamar said, and Amr ibn Maymun ibn Mihran said to me, and I asked him about that, and he said: They only made treaty upon their religion; meaning: they are not demolished."
"18598","My uncle Wahb ibn Nafi informed us, he said: I witnessed the letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz to Urwah ibn Muhammad: that the old churches be demolished. I saw him demolishing them, then they were restored. When Raja came, he called me, and I testified to the letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, so he demolished them a second time."
"18599","Ibn al-Taymi informed us, from his father, he said: A shaykh of the people of Madinah called Hanash Abu Ali narrated to me, that Ikrimah informed him, he said: Ibn Abbas was asked: May the polytheists take churches in the land of the Arabs? Ibn Abbas said: As for what the Muslims have founded as cities, no church, no synagogue, no cross, no spearhead is to be raised in it; no horn is blown in it, no bell is struck in it; neither wine nor pork may enter it. And whatever land they were given a treaty of peace upon, the Muslims must fulfill the treaty for them. The explanation of 'what the Muslims founded': meaning what was of their land or what they took by force."
"18600","Mamar informed us, he said: Amr ibn Maymun ibn Mihran informed me, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote that the Christians in Syria be prevented from striking bells. He said: And they were forbidden to part their hair (like others), and he ordered their forelocks to be cut, and that they fasten their girdles, and not ride upon saddles, and not wear turbans of embroidered cloth nor silk, and not raise their crosses above their churches. If anyone of them is found doing any of that after warning, his spoil belongs to whoever finds him. He said: And he wrote that their women be prevented from riding litters."
"18601","From al-Thawri, from Simak, from Qabus, from his father, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr wrote to Ali asking him about a Muslim who committed adultery with a Christian woman. He wrote back to him: Carry out the hadd punishment upon the Muslim, and return the Christian woman to the people of her religion."
"18602","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ata said: We have the choice; if we wish we judge among them, and if we wish we do not judge. If we judge, we judge among them by our judgment among ourselves, and we leave them to their judgment among themselves. That is the meaning of His saying: 'And judge among them.' And Amr ibn Shuayb said the same; that is the meaning of His saying: 'So judge among them or turn away from them.'"
"18603","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Sunnah has passed that they be referred in their rights and inheritances to the people of their religion, unless they come desiring a hadd judgment in which we judge among them; then we judge among them by the Book of Allah. Allah said to His Messenger: 'And if you judge, judge among them with justice.'"
"18604","Al-Thawri informed us, from al-Suddi, from Ikrimah, he said: His saying: 'Judge among them by what Allah sent down' abrogated His saying: 'So judge among them or turn away from them.'"
"18605","Al-Thawri informed us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim and Amir, they both said: If the ruler wishes he judges among them, and if he wishes he turns away from them; if he judges among them, he judges by what Allah sent down."
"18606","Mamar informed us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, that Umar wrote to Adi ibn Adi: When the People of the Book come to you, judge among them."
"18607","Mamar informed us, from Ibn Shubrumah, he said: I saw al-Shabi carrying out a hadd punishment upon a Jew for slander, in the mosque, while he wore a shirt."
"18608","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: If a man of the People of the Book commits adultery with a Muslim woman, or steals something from a Muslim, the punishment is carried out upon him, and the imam does not turn away from that. They say: In everything between the Muslims and them, he does not turn away from it."
"18609","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ismail ibn Muhammad, and Yaqub ibn Utbah, and others: they claimed there is no hadd upon one who accuses them (of adultery), except that the ruler may chastise him."
"18610","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah informed me, from his father, he said: I asked him: Is there a hadd upon one who slanders the dhimmis? He said: I do not see any hadd upon him. Ibn Jurayj said: And I heard Nafi saying: There is no hadd upon him."
"18611","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: There is no hadd upon one who accuses a Jew or Christian (of adultery)."
"18612","Al-Thawri informed us, from Tariq ibn Abd al-Rahman and Mutarrif ibn Tarif, they said: We were with al-Shabi when two men, a Muslim and a Christian, were brought to him; each had slandered the other. He flogged the Christian eighty lashes for (slandering) the Muslim, and said to the Christian (about the Muslim's slander): There is nothing in you greater than this slander of him, so he left him. That was reported to Abd al-Hamid, who wrote about it to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz mentioning what al-Shabi did, and Umar wrote approving the deed of al-Shabi."
"18613","Al-Thawri said: Whoever slanders a Jew or Christian, there is no hadd upon him. If a Christian slanders a Christian woman, some of them are not flogged for others when they dispute to the people of Islam, just as no Muslim is flogged for them when he slanders them; likewise some of them are not flogged for others."
"18614","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ata said to me: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have been commanded to fight them until they say: There is no god but Allah; when they say it, their blood and property are protected, except by its right, and their reckoning is upon Allah."
"18615","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Fight the people until they say: There is no god but Allah; when they do that, their blood and property are protected, except by its right, and their reckoning is upon Allah."
"18616","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I asked Ata: Are the Magians People of the Book? He said: No. I said: What about the Asbadhiyyun? He said: A letter of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to them was found, they claimed, after Umar intended to take jizyah from them; when he found it, he left them. He said: They claimed that."
"18617","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Jafar ibn Muhammad informed me, from his father, that Umar ibn al-Khattab went out and passed by some Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), among them Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf. He said: I do not know what to do with these people who are neither Arabs nor People of the Book, meaning the Magians. Abd al-Rahman said: I bear witness that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Treat them with the practice of the People of the Book."
"18618","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Jafar also informed me, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to the people of Hajar: That no good-doer shall bear the sin of an evil-doer, and: If I were to strive against you, I would expel you from Hajar."
"18619","Mamar informed us, he said: I heard al-Zuhri being asked: Is jizyah taken from one who is not of the People of the Book? He said: Yes; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it from the people of Bahrayn, and Umar from the people of the Sawad, and Uthman from the Berbers."
"18620","Al-Thawri informed us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to the Magians of Hajar calling them to Islam; whoever accepted Islam, the truth (zakat etc.) was accepted from him, and whoever refused, jizyah was imposed upon him, and that their slaughtered animals not be eaten, and their women not be married."
"18621","Mamar informed us, from Qatadah and others: that twenty-four dirhams per year used to be taken from the Magians of Bahrayn, from each man."
"18622","Al-Thawri informed us, from Qays ibn Muhammad, or Muhammad ibn Qays, from al-Shabi, he said: The people of the Sawad had no covenant; when kharaj was taken from them, they gained a covenant."
"18623","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made peace with the idol-worshippers upon jizyah, except those of them who were Arabs; and he accepted jizyah from the people of Bahrayn, and they were Magians."
"18624","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Yaqub ibn Utbah, and Ismail ibn Muhammad, and others, that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took jizyah from the Magians of Hajar, and that Umar ibn al-Khattab took from the Magians of the Sawad, and that Uthman took from the Berbers."
"18625","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Bajalah al-Tamimi, that Umar ibn al-Khattab did not wish to take jizyah from the Magians until Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf testified that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it from the Magians of Hajar."
"18626","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from a shaykh of theirs called Abu Sad, from a man who witnessed that, I think Nasr ibn Asim, that al-Mustawrid ibn Alqamah was in a gathering, or Farwah ibn Nawfal al-Ashjai, and a man said: There is no jizyah upon the Magians. Al-Mustawrid said: Do you say this, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it from the Magians of Hajar? By Allah, what you concealed is more wicked than what you revealed. He took him until they entered upon Ali, who was in a palace sitting in a dome. He said: O Commander of the Faithful, this one claims there is no jizyah upon the Magians, though you know the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it from the Magians of Hajar. Ali said: To us he says: Sit down. By Allah, there is no one on earth today more knowledgeable about that than I. The Magians were People of a Book they knew, and knowledge they studied. Their chief drank wine and fell upon his sister; a group of Muslims saw him. In the morning his sister said: You did such-and-such with her, and a group saw you who will not conceal for you. He summoned the greedy people and gave them gifts, then said to them: You know that Adam married his sons to his daughters. Then those who had seen him came and said: Woe to the outcast, there is a hadd upon your back. So he killed them, and they were those with him. Then a woman came and said to him: Yes, I saw you. He said to her: Woe to the prostitute of such-and-such tribe. She said: Yes, by Allah, I was a prostitute, then I repented. So he killed her. Then what was in their hearts and their books was taken away by night, and nothing remained sound with them."
"18627","Mamar informed us, from Qatadah and others: that twenty-four dirhams per year used to be taken from the Magians of Bahrayn, from each man."
"18628","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I asked Ata about jizyah, and he said: We know nothing except what they were reconciled upon; then they protected all their property."
"18629","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Musa ibn Uqbah informed me, from Nafi, that he narrated from Umar: that he imposed jizyah upon every male who reached puberty, forty dirhams or four dinars. He imposed silver upon those of them in Iraq, for it is a land of silver, and imposed gold upon the people of Syria and Egypt, for it is a land of gold. And he imposed upon them besides that the provisions and clothing of the Muslims which Umar used to clothe the people with, and hospitality for any Muslim who lodged with them, three nights and their days."
"18630","Ibn Jurayj said: Musa said; Nafi said: I heard Aslam, the freedman of Umar, narrating to Ibn Umar: that the people of jizyah from Syria came to Umar and said: When the Muslims lodge with us, they burden us with sheep and chickens. Umar said: Feed them from your food which you eat, and do not add to that for them."
"18631","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Aslam, that Umar imposed the jizyah and wrote to the commanders of the armies not to impose jizyah except upon those over whom the razor has passed, and not to impose it upon a child or a woman. He imposed upon the people of Iraq forty dirhams upon each man, and also fifteen sas upon them; and he imposed upon the people of Syria four dinars upon each man, and also two mudds of wheat and three qists of oil, and such-and-such of honey and fat, which Ayyub or Nafi did not remember; and he imposed upon the people of Egypt four dinars upon each man of them, and an irdabb of wheat upon them, and something he does not remember, and the clothing of the Commander of the Faithful as a fixed levy; and upon them is hospitality for the Muslims for three days, feeding them from what they eat of what is lawful for the Muslims of their food. When Umar came to Syria, they complained to him that they burden them with chickens. Umar said: Do not feed them except from what you eat of what is lawful for them of your food."
"18632","Mamar informed us, from al-Amash, from Shaqiq ibn Salamah, from Masruq ibn al-Ajda, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Muadh to Yemen and ordered him to take jizyah from every adult male and female, a dinar or its value in maafiri cloth."
"18633","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from a man of Banu Ghifar, he said: Umar said: Do not buy the slaves of the dhimmis, for they are people of kharaj, some of whom pay on behalf of others; meaning their lands."
"18634","Al-Thawri said: That is left to the ruler; he increases upon them according to their ease and reduces from them according to their need. There is no fixed time for it; the ruler considers according to what they can bear. As for what was not taken by force until they were reconciled by treaty, nothing is added upon them beyond what they were reconciled upon. The jizyah is upon what they were reconciled upon, little or much, in their lands and upon their persons. He says: There is no zakat upon them in their property."
"18635","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Ibn Abi Najih, he said: I said to Mujahid: Why is jizyah taken from the People of the Book of Syria as four dinars, and from the people of Yemen as one dinar? He said: That is due to affluence."
"18636","Al-Thawri informed us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from a man of Juhaynah among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perhaps you will fight a people and prevail over them, and they will shield themselves from you with their wealth rather than their persons and children, and make peace with you; so take nothing from them beyond that."
"18637","Abdullah ibn Umar informed us, from Nafi, from Aslam the freedman of Umar, that Umar wrote to the commanders of the armies not to impose jizyah upon women nor upon children, and to impose jizyah upon the adult males over whom the razor has passed; and to seal upon their necks, cut their forelocks for whoever of them takes hair, bind girdles upon them, and forbid them riding except upon packsaddles sideways. He says: meaning his two legs on one side. Abdullah said: And Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz did that with them when he ruled. And Abdullah said in the hadith of Nafi, from Aslam: And Umar imposed jizyah upon those of them in Syria, four dinars upon each man, and two mudds of food, and two or three qists of oil; and he imposed upon those in Egypt four dinars, and two irdabbs of food, and something he mentioned; and he imposed upon those in Iraq forty dirhams and fifteen qafizes, and something we do not remember; and he imposed upon them besides that hospitality for any Muslim who passes by them for three days, and he imposed garments upon them, and he mentioned honey which we did not remember."
"18638","Al-Khurasani informed us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to me, he said: Khalid ibn Abi Imran informed me, that Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to him, that a man seized a feedbag containing grass and something, taking it from the dhimmis. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the man: Take this! He said: I took it and it is nothing. He said: Have you violated my protection, have you violated the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? So the man went and gave it to its owner, then came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he took it (his pledge). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Did you not need what you took? He said: Yes. He said: Then the one you took it for needs it more."
"18639","Ibn Abi Layla informed us: that an army passed by the crop of a dhimmi man and let their beasts loose in it. One man of them restrained his beast, keeping it to the pasture and preventing it from the crop. The dhimmi came to the one who restrained his beast and said: May Allah suffice for you, or: May Allah suffice me through you; had it not been for you, these would have sufficed (harmed); but you ward off these through you."
"18640","Mamar informed us, from Qatadah, from Abu Mijlaz, that Umar ibn al-Khattab sent Ammar ibn Yasir, Abdullah ibn Masud, and Uthman ibn Hunayf to Kufah. He put Ammar over prayer and fighting, Abdullah over judiciary and the treasury, and Uthman ibn Hunayf over land survey. He assigned them each day a sheep: half of it and its offal for Ammar, a quarter for Ibn Masud, and a quarter for Ibn Hunayf. Then he said: I do not see a town from which a sheep is taken every day but that will quickly wear it out. Then he said: I have settled you and myself from this wealth like the guardian of the orphan: whoever is rich, let him abstain, and whoever is poor, let him eat in a fair manner. Then Uthman levied upon every head of the dhimmis twenty-four dirhams for each year, and imposed nothing of that upon women and children. Then he surveyed the Sawad of the people of Kufah, of the land of the dhimmis, and put upon the jarib of date-palms ten dirhams, upon the jarib of grapes eight dirhams, upon the jarib of sugar-cane six dirhams, upon the jarib of wheat four dirhams, and upon the jarib of barley two dirhams. Umar approved of that."
"18641","Mamar informed us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that Ibrahim ibn Sad, who was a governor at Aden, asked Ibn Abbas: What is due in the wealth of the dhimmah? He said: Pardon (nothing fixed). He said: They order us with such-and-such? He said: Then do not act for them. I said: What about ambergris? He said: If there is anything in it, it is the fifth."
"18642","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Ruzayq the collector of customs in Egypt, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to him: Whoever passes by you of the Muslims with property he trades with, take from him his sadaqah: from every forty dinars a dinar; what is less than that down to twenty, by that reckoning; if it falls short by a third of a dinar, take nothing from him. And whoever passes by you of the People of the Book and dhimmis who trade, take from him from every twenty dinars a dinar; what is less, by that reckoning down to ten dinars; if it falls short by a third of a dinar, take nothing from him."
"18643","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Yahya ibn Said also informed me, that the first to take half the tenths from the dhimmis when they traded was Umar ibn al-Khattab; he used to take from the Nabatean merchants of Syria when they came to Madinah."
"18644","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: The people of Manbij and beyond the Sea of Aden wrote to Umar ibn al-Khattab offering that they enter with their trade into the land of the Arabs, with a tenth of it for him. Umar asked the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they agreed upon that. He was the first to take tenths from them."
"18645","Mamar informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: Double what is taken from the Muslims of gold and silver is taken from the People of the Book. Umar ibn al-Khattab did that, and Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz."
"18646","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, that Umar used to take from the Nabateans, of wheat and oil, a tenth, intending thereby to increase carriage; and he took from legumes half a tenth; meaning chickpeas, lentils, and the like."
"18647","Said ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Tanukhi informed us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi Ablah informed me, he said: I had land with its jizyah. My agent wrote about it to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, and Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote: Collect the jizyah and the tenths, then take from him the excess; meaning: take from him whichever of the two is greater."
"18648","Mamar informed us, from Ali ibn al-Hakam al-Bunani, from Muhammad ibn Zayd, from Ibrahim al-Nakhai, that a man accepted Islam in the time of Umar ibn al-Khattab and said: Lift the jizyah from my land. Umar said to him: Your land was taken by force. He said: And a man came to Umar ibn al-Khattab and said: The people of such-and-such land of mine can bear more kharaj than is upon them. He said: There is no way to them; they were only reconciled by treaty."
"18649","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that a man of the people of Najran accepted Islam, and they wanted to take jizyah from him, or as he said; so he refused. Umar said: You are but seeking refuge. The man said: There is indeed a refuge in Islam, if I do (embrace) it. Umar said: You have spoken the truth, by Allah! There is indeed a refuge in Islam."
"18650","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: It is not fitting for a Muslim to pay jizyah, admitting smallness and humiliation. I heard more than one mentioning that."
"18651","Al-Thawri informed us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas when a man came to him and said: I take land, undertaking kharaj land as a tenant, cultivating it and paying its kharaj. He forbade him. Then another came to him and he forbade him. Then another came to him and he forbade him. Then he said: Do not deliberately take what Allah has put upon this disbeliever, removing it from his neck and putting it upon your neck. Then he recited: Fight those who do not believe in Allah, until humbled."
"18652","Al-Thawri informed us, from Kulayb ibn Wail, he said: I asked Ibn Umar: What do you think about buying land? He said: It is good. I said: They take from me from every jarib a qafiz and a dirham. He said: Smallness is put upon your neck."
"18653","Al-Thawri informed us, from Jafar ibn Burqan, he said: Maymun ibn Mihran informed us, he said: I heard Ibn Umar saying: I would not like all the land to be mine as jizyah land for five dirhams, admitting smallness therein."
"18654","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote: Do not buy of the real estate of the dhimmis, nor of their lands, anything."
"18655","Mamar informed us, from one who heard al-Hasan, he said: Regarding the apostate, his inheritance belongs to the Muslims; and they used to make it lawful for his heirs. He said: And Qatadah said: His inheritance belongs to the people of his religion."
"18656","Mamar informed us, from Ishaq ibn Rashid, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote regarding a Muslim man taken captive who became Christian: When that is known, his wife is quit of him, observes waiting of three courses from him, and his property is paid to his Muslim heirs."
"18657","Al-Thawri informed us: Regarding the apostate, when he is killed his property belongs to his heirs; when he joins the land of war his property belongs to the Muslims. I know him only to have said: Except that he has an heir upon his religion in a land, who has more right to it."
"18658","Al-Thawri informed us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, that Umar said: The people of shirk, we inherit from them but they do not inherit from us."
"18659","Abd al-Malik al-Dhimari informed us, from al-Thawri, from Musa ibn Abi Kathir, he said: I asked Ibn al-Musayyib about the apostate: how long does his wife observe waiting? He said: Three courses. I said: He was killed? He said: Then four months and ten days. I said: Is his inheritance connected? He said: His inheritance is not connected. I said: Do his sons inherit him? He said: We inherit from them but they do not inherit from us."
"18660","Mamar informed us, from al-Amash, from Abu Amr al-Shaybani, he said: An old man who had been Christian, then accepted Islam, then apostatized from Islam, was brought to Ali. Ali said to him: Perhaps you apostatized only to obtain an inheritance, then return to Islam? He said: No. He said: Then return to Islam. He said: Not until I meet Christ, no. So Ali ordered him and his neck was struck, and his inheritance was paid to his Muslim children. Mamar informed us, and Ibn Jurayj, they both said: It reached us that Ibn Masud said regarding the inheritance of the apostate like the saying of Ali."
"18661","Mamar informed us, Qatadah said: His inheritance belongs to the people of his religion."
"18662","Abd al-Malik al-Dhimari informed us, from al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Ubayd, from al-Hasan, he said: The Muslims used to make the inheritance of the apostate lawful for his heirs."
"18663","Abd al-Malik al-Dhimari informed us, from al-Thawri, he said: It reached us that Ali gave the heirs of Mustawrid al-Ijli his property."
"18664","Abdullah ibn Said informed us, from al-Hajjaj, from al-Hakam, that Ali said: The inheritance of the apostate belongs to his child."
"18665","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: People are two groups: a group says the inheritance of the apostate belongs to the Muslims, because the moment he disbelieves it is suspended from him; nothing of it can be taken until it is seen whether he accepts Islam or disbelieves. Among them are al-Nakhai, al-Shabi, and al-Hakam ibn Utaybah. And a group says: It belongs to the people of his religion."
"18666","Ibn Jurayj informed us, Ata said to me: No Muslim inherits a disbeliever, and no disbeliever inherits a Muslim. And Amr ibn Dinar said the same."
"18667","Mamar informed us, and Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Ali ibn Husayn, from Amr ibn Uthman, from Usamah ibn Zayd, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Muslim does not inherit the disbeliever, nor the disbeliever the Muslim."
"18668","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: People of two different religions do not inherit from one another."
"18669","He said: And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed: Muslims and Christians do not inherit from one another; and so did Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman."
"18670","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Maymun ibn Mihran informed me, from a man of Kindah called al-Urs ibn Qays, he said: An old man who was employed over al-Hirah informed me, that he informed him that al-Ashath ibn Qays had a Jewish paternal aunt who died. He came to Umar ibn al-Khattab seeking her inheritance. Umar refused to give him inheritance from her, and gave her inheritance to the Jews."
"18671","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Yahya ibn Said informed me, he said: I heard Sulayman ibn Yasar mentioning, that Muhammad ibn al-Ashath informed him, that a paternal aunt of his died Jewish. Al-Ashath mentioned that to Umar, and he said: None inherits her but the people of her religion."
"18672","Mamar informed us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: People of two different religions do not inherit from one another."
"18673","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, or another, that Umar said: The people of (other) faiths do not inherit, nor do they inherit from us."
"18674","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: Neither Jews nor Christians inherit Muslims, nor are they inherited by them, except that he be a man's slave or bondwoman."
"18675","Mamar informed us, he said: One who heard Ikrimah informed me: he was asked about a man who freed a Christian slave of his; the slave died leaving property. He said: His inheritance belongs to the people of his religion."
"18676","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I was told from Makhul, he said: If a Christian slave of yours dies, and you find with him gold in cash from the price of wine and pigs, take it; if you find wine or pigs, do not. If he has no relatives, the Muslim inherits him by Islam."
"18677","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Ali ibn Husayn, that Abu Talib was inherited by Aqil and Talib, and neither Ali nor Jafar inherited him, because they were both Muslims."
"18678","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Amr ibn Shuayb, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): That the Muslim does not inherit the disbeliever, while he has a relative of his (the deceased's) religion."
"18679","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Amr ibn Shuayb, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): That the Muslim does not inherit the disbeliever, while he has a relative of his religion; if he has no heir, the Muslim inherits him by Islam."
"18680","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ata and Ibn Abi Layla said: If a Muslim dies leaving Christian children, and his property is not divided until his Christian children accept Islam, they have no right; the shares were fixed before they accepted Islam. He said: And likewise the slave whose free father dies, and his inheritance is not divided until he is freed. Abd al-Malik ibn al-Sabbah informed us, from al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, the same."
"18681","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, he said: I heard Abu al-Sha'tha saying: If a Muslim dies leaving a Muslim and a disbelieving son, and his inheritance is not divided until the disbeliever accepts Islam, he inherits it with the believer; they inherit together. It did not please me."
"18682","Mamar informed us, he said: I heard al-Zuhri saying: When the shares of inheritance are fixed, whoever accepts Islam upon an inheritance gets nothing."
"18683","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from a man who wrote to him: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. To proceed: You wrote to me to send Yazid ibn Qatadah al-Anazi. I asked him, and he said: My mother died Christian while I am Muslim. She left thirty slaves and bondwomen, and two hundred date-palms. We rode concerning that to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he decreed that her inheritance belongs to her husband and her nephew, who are both Christians, and he gave me nothing. Yazid ibn Qatadah said: My grandfather died while Muslim; he had pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and witnessed Hunayn with him. He left his daughter; Uthman gave me all his property and gave his daughter nothing. I held the property for a year or two, then his daughter accepted Islam. I rode to Uthman; he asked Abdullah ibn al-Arqam, who said to him: Umar used to decree that whoever accepts Islam upon an inheritance before it is divided has an obligatory inheritance by his Islam. So Uthman gave her her share anew. All of that while I was present."
"18684","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ata said to me when I asked him: If there are two Christians with small children, and one of them accepts Islam, and their children die leaving property, their Muslim father does not inherit them; rather their mother inherits them, and what remains belongs to the people of their religion. I said: They are small, they have no religion? He said: But they were born into Christianity upon Christianity. And indeed he once said to me: The Muslim inherits from them the inheritance from his two parents. I know him only to have said: Their small child inherits them and they inherit him, until a religion unites them or separates. And I mentioned them to Amr ibn Dinar. I said: Their parents are Christians? He said: I would give their property to their child. I said to Amr: How, when the child is upon the fitrah? He said: Then why are the children of the polytheists taken captive when they are upon the fitrah, while they are Muslims? So I was silent."
"18685","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I heard Sulayman ibn Musa informing Ata, he said: The matter that passed among our predecessors, which is acted upon, about which we have no doubt, and which we hold, is that two Christians with their small child between them: they inherit him and he inherits them, until a religion separates them or unites. If his mother accepts Islam, she inherits him by the Book of Allah, and what remains belongs to the Muslims. If his parents are Christians and he is small, and he has a Muslim brother or Muslim sister through his mother, his brother or sister inherits him by the Book of Allah, then what remains belongs to the Muslims. He said: And prayer is not performed over the children of the Christians, nor are they followed to their graves; they are buried in their graveyard. If a Muslim kills one of their children deliberately, he is not killed for him, and his blood-money is the blood-money of Christians."
"18686","Ibn Jurayj informed us: I said to Sulayman: A small child between two polytheists, then one of them and their child accept Islam; the small child, then their father dies? He said: Their Muslim child inherits from his two parents, and the disbeliever of them inherits nothing; inheritance then is between the child and the Muslim, and the disbeliever then inherits nothing of his two parents."
"18687","Mamar informed us, from a man, from al-Hasan, and from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, they both said: The Muslim of the two has most right to him; they inherit him and he inherits them. Al-Thawri informed us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, the same."
"18688","Al-Thawri said: Regarding a Christian who dies while his wife is pregnant, then she accepts Islam before giving birth, then gives birth and dies: he said: Their child inherits them both entirely, because the inheritance of his father fell to him when his father died; then his mother died, so he followed her upon her religion, and inherited her."
"18689","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, he said: Safiyyah, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), sold a house of hers to Muawiyah for one hundred thousand. She said to a Jewish relative of hers: Accept Islam, for if you accept Islam you will inherit me. He refused, so she bequeathed to him. One of them said: Thirty thousand."
"18690","Ibn Jurayj said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla said to me: Regarding a Jewish household whose father died and his inheritance was not divided until they accepted Islam: it is not upon the division of Islam; the shares were fixed before they accepted Islam."
"18691","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu al-Sha'tha, he said: When a man dies leaving his son a slave, and he is freed before the inheritance is divided, it is his; meaning: he inherits."
"18692","Mamar informed us, from Ibn Tawus, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, and Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir ibn Zayd, they both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whatever division was in Jahiliyyah stands upon the division of Jahiliyyah; and what Islam finds undivided is upon the division of Islam. Mamar informed us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, from Nafi, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"18693","Mamar informed us, from Qatadah, and from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Whoever accepts Islam upon an inheritance before it is divided inherits from it."
"18694","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: When a man dies leaving his son a slave, and he is freed before the inheritance is divided, he has nothing."
"18695","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I and Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla said: If a Magian marries his daughter and she bears him two daughters, then he dies, then they accept Islam, then one of the two daughters of his daughter dies: the full sister gets half, the mother gets a sixth, veiled herself because she is the sister of her daughter, and veiled by her surviving daughter, the sister of her daughter; then the mother also gets what belongs to the sister through the father. And al-Thawri said like their saying: The full sister gets half, the sister through the father gets a sixth completing two-thirds, and she is the mother; and she gets a sixth as mother, veiling herself, and because she is a sister; so a third fell to her."
"18696","Mamar informed us, from Qatadah, and Amr ibn Ubayd, they both said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to Adi ibn Artah to ask al-Hasan ibn Ali about the Magians and mothers: so I asked him, and he said: And the marriage of sisters, the shirk they are upon is greater than that; they were only left to it because of the jizyah."
"18697","Al-Thawri informed us, from al-Shabi, that Ali and Ibn Masud said: Regarding the Magian, he inherits from two places."
"18698","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri: Regarding the Magian, he said: We give them inheritance by the nearest kinship to him. Al-Thawri said regarding a Magian who married his sister and she bore him a daughter, then they accepted Islam and he died: he said: His daughter inherits half, and half belongs to his sister because she is a paternal relative. And he said regarding a Magian who married his mother and she bore two daughters, then they accepted Islam, and the man died: his two daughters get two-thirds, his mother a sixth; then one of the two daughters died: her daughter inherits half, and the mother became a mother and grandmother, so she veiled herself; so we gave her the inheritance of the mother, and we do not give her the inheritance of the grandmother. We say: Because when they accepted Islam the marriage bond was dissolved; he should not remain after Islam upon his mother nor upon his sister, and we gave inheritance by kinship."
"18699","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I said to Ata: What is His saying: 'Except that you do kindness to your allies'? He said: Giving. I said: The giving of the believer to the disbeliever between whom is kinship? He said: Yes, his giving to him alive, and his bequest to him."
"18700","Mamar informed us, from Qatadah: Regarding His saying: 'Except that you do kindness to your allies': he said: Except that you have a relative not upon your religion, and you bequeath to him something; he is your ally by lineage, but not your ally in religion. And al-Hasan said the same."
"18701","Hisham ibn Urwah informed me, from his father, from Asma bint Abi Bakr, she said: My mother came while a polytheist, during the treaty of Quraysh when they treated with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and their truce. I sought a ruling from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: My mother has come, desiring; shall I join her? He said: Yes, join your mother."
"18702","Al-Thawri informed us, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: The bequest of the Muslim to the Christian is valid."
"18703","Al-Thawri informed us, from Layth, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that Safiyyah, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), bequeathed to a Christian relative of hers."
"18704","Al-Thawri said: No bequest to the people of war is valid."
"18705","Al-Thawri informed us, from Layth, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that Safiyyah, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), bequeathed to a Jewish relative of hers."
"18706","Ibn Jurayj reported: I said to Ata: Is a Muslim slave sold to a disbeliever? He said: No, as a matter of personal opinion. And Amr ibn Dinar said to me: No, as a matter of personal opinion."
"18707","Ibn Jurayj said, and I heard Sulayman ibn Musa saying: In our view, a disbeliever does not hold a Muslim in slavery."
"18708","Ibn Jurayj reported: Ibn Shihab was asked about a Christian who had a Christian slave-girl of his, and she bore him a child, then she accepted Islam. He said: Islam separates between them, and she and her child are set free."
"18709","Ibn Jurayj said, and I heard Sulayman ibn Musa saying: In his view, a disbeliever does not hold a Muslim in slavery."
"18710","Al-Thawri said concerning the slave-mother of a child belonging to a Christian, who accepts Islam, he said: She pays off her own value, working to earn her price, and she is kept apart from him; if he dies she is free, and if he accepts Islam after she has begun earning, she continues earning and does not return to him, and whether he dies as a Christian or a Muslim, no earning is due upon her. And al-Thawri said: Concerning the mudabbar slave of a Christian who accepts Islam, the same as he said about his umm walad."
"18711","Maamar and al-Thawri reported from Amr ibn Maymun, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote concerning the slaves of the dhimmis who accept Islam, ordering that they be sold. Al-Thawri said: And likewise we say: they are to be sold."
"18712","Al-Thawri said concerning a man in whose possession a slave accepts Islam, and he conceals him or hides him away, he said: He is disciplined and the slave is sold."
"18713","Ibn Jurayj reported: One of the trustworthy people informed me that a Christian freed a Muslim slave. Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz said: Give him his value from the public treasury, and his wala is for the Muslims."
"18714","Al-Thawri was asked about Muslim merchants who enter the lands of the non-Arabs, where some of them enslave others: is it proper for one who knows that to buy them? He said: Yes."
"18715","Ibn Jurayj reported: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz said: When a Jew frees a Muslim, he is given his value from the public treasury, and his wala is for the Muslims."
"18716","Ibn Jurayj reported: Ibn Shihab said concerning a man of the People of the Book who secretly bought a Muslim slave-girl, and she bore him a child, he said: He is banished/punished and she is taken away from him."
"18717","Ibn Jurayj reported: Ata said to me: No polytheist enters the entire Haram, and he recited: after this year of theirs. Ibn Jurayj said: And Ata said to me, His saying the Sacred Mosque means the entire Haram. Ibn Jurayj said: And Amr ibn Dinar said the same: He does not enter the Sacred Mosque."
"18718","Ibn Jurayj reported: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah saying concerning this verse: The polytheists are only impure, so let them not approach the Sacred Mosque, he said: No, except if he is a slave, or one of the people of the jizya."
"18719","Maamar reported from Ibn Abi Najih, he said: I lived at a time when no Jew or Christian was left to enter the Haram."
"18720","Maamar reported from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two religions shall not coexist in the land of the Arabs, or he said: in the land of the Hijaz. Al-Zuhri said: That is why Umar expelled them."
"18721","Maamar reported from Ayyub, from Nafi, he said: Umar would not let a Jew, a Christian, or a Magian who entered Madina stay in it except for three days, enough to sell their goods. When Umar was struck, he said: I had commanded you not to bring in anyone of them to us, and had the stricken one been other than me, he would have had a decision in it. And he would say: Two religions shall not coexist in it."
"18722","Maamar reported from Ayyub, he said: When Umar was stabbed, he sent for some of the Emigrants, among them Ali, and said: Was this with the approval of your notables? Ali said: Allah forbid that it should be with approval from us; had we been able to add to your life from our own lifetimes, we would have done so. He said: I had forbidden you that anyone of them should enter to us."
"18723","Ibn Jurayj reported from Musa ibn Uqba, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Jews and Christians and others of the disbelievers who came to Madina as travelers would not stay in it more than three days in the time of Umar, and we do not know whether that was done with them before or not."
"18724","Ibn Jurayj reported from Muslim ibn Abi Maryam, from Ali ibn Husayn, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) expelled the Jews from Madina."
"18725","Ibn Jurayj reported from Musa ibn Uqba, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that the Jews of Banu al-Nadir and Qurayza fought against the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he expelled Banu al-Nadir, and left Qurayza and those under them, until Qurayza fought him afterwards; then he killed their men, and divided their women, children, and property among the Muslims, except that some of them joined the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he granted them safety and they accepted Islam. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) expelled all the Jews of Madina: Banu Qaynuqa, who are the people of Abd Allah ibn Salam, and the Jews of Banu Haritha, and every Jew who was in Madina."
"18726","Ibn Jurayj reported: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah saying: Umar ibn al-Khattab informed me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: I shall surely expel the Jews and the Christians from the Arabian Peninsula, until I leave none in it but a Muslim."
"18727","Ibn Jurayj reported: Musa ibn Uqba informed me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that Umar expelled the Jews and Christians from the land of the Hijaz. And when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prevailed over Khaybar, he wanted to expel the Jews from it, and the land when he prevailed over it belonged to Allah and His Messenger and the Muslims. So he intended to expel the Jews from it, and the Jews asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to let them stay in it on condition that they do its work for him, with half the produce for them. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: We let you stay in it on that basis for as long as we wish. So they stayed in it until Umar expelled them to Tayma and Ariha."
"18728","Maamar reported from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two religions shall not coexist in the land of the Arabs, or he said: in the land of the Hijaz. He said: So Umar investigated that until he found confirmation of it. Al-Zuhri said: That is why Umar expelled them."
"18729","Malik reported from Ismail ibn Abi Hakim, that he heard Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz saying: The last words spoken by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were that he said: May Allah fight the Jews and the Christians; they took the graves of their prophets as mosques. No two religions shall remain, or he said: shall coexist, in the land of the Arabs."
"18730","Maamar reported from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) handed Khaybar to the Jews on condition that they work in it, with half its produce for them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Abu Bakr, and the early part of Umars caliphate, ruled according to that."
"18731","Then Umar was informed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in the illness in which he died: Two religions shall not coexist in the land of the Arabs, or he said: in the land of the Hijaz. So he investigated that until he found confirmation of it, then he summoned them and said: Whoever has a covenant from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), let him bring it; otherwise, I shall expel you. So he expelled them from it."
"18732","Ibn Uyayna reported from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab heard a Jewish man saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: It is as if I see you, with your saddle laid upon your camel, travelling night after night. So Umar said: By Allah, you shall not keep walking with it. The Jew said: By Allah, I have never seen a word harder upon the one who spoke it, nor easier upon the one to whom it was said, than it."
"18733","Ibn Uyayna reported from Sulayman al-Ahwal, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: Ibn Abbas said: Thursday, and what is Thursday? Then he wept until his tears soaked the pebbles. I said: O Abu Abbas, what is Thursday? He said: The pain of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) grew severe, so he said: Bring me something to write for you a writing after which you shall never go astray. He said: So they disputed, and disputing is not proper in the presence of a prophet. They said: What is wrong with him? Ask him to make himself understood; is he delirious? He said: Leave me, for what I am in is better than what you call me to. He said: Then at his death he enjoined three things, saying: Expel the polytheists from the Arabian Peninsula, and honor the delegations in the manner I used to honor them. He said: Either Said fell silent about the third, or he said it and I forgot it."
"18734","Ibn Jurayj reported: It reached me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoined at his death that no Jew or Christian be left in the Hijaz, and that the army of Usama be sent forth to Syria, and he enjoined good treatment of the Copts, for they have kinship."
"18735","Al-Hasan ibn Umara reported from Adi ibn Thabit, from Abu Zabyan, he said: I heard Ali saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: When you are given authority after me, expel the people of Najran from the Arabian Peninsula."
"18736","He said: And Ibn al-Taymi informed us, from Layth, from Tawus, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas saying: The Jews and Christians shall not share your towns with you unless they accept Islam; whoever of them apostatizes and refuses, nothing less than his blood is accepted from him."
"18737","Maamar reported from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you come to rule over the Copts, treat them well, for they have a covenant of protection and kinship. Maamar said: I said to al-Zuhri: Does he mean the mother of Ibrahim, the son of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: No, rather the mother of Ismail."
"18738","Maamar reported, he said: I was with Yahya ibn Abi Kathir at Yamama, and I wanted to go out, and on the road there was a stretch of desert, and I found no one. So he went out to some Jews and came to them, and commended me to them. When I travelled with them, they said to me on the road: How did Yahya send you with us, when it is narrated from your Prophet that no Jew is alone with a Muslim but he intends to kill him? He said: So I feared them, but Allah kept me safe from them."
"18739","Al-Thawri reported: And he was asked about non-Arab slaves who come out of the sea or from elsewhere: may they be sold to Jews and Christians? He said: If they are adults, Islam is offered to them; if they accept Islam, that is it, otherwise they are sold to Jews and Christians if their owner wishes. What is recommended in that is: when a Muslim comes to own Jews and Christians by purchase or captivity, he calls them to Islam; if they refuse but to cling to their religion, then the Muslim, if he wishes, sells them to the people of the covenant, and he does not sell them to anyone of the people of war. If they are of no religion, like the Indians and the Zanj, the Muslim does not sell them to anyone of the people of the covenant nor of the people of war, and he sells them only to Muslims, for they respond when called, and they have no religion to cling to, and the Jews and Christians should not be left to Judaize or Christianize them. If the non-Arabs are children, they are not sold to Jews and Christians; they are sold only to Muslims, and if they die as children in the care of a Muslim, he prays over them, even if he did not bring them out of their land; he prays over them once they fall into his hands. Al-Thawri said: And Hammad said: When a child is owned, he is a Muslim."
"18740","Abd al-Malik ibn al-Sabbah reported from al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from Awf ibn Malik al-Ashjai: that a Jew or a Christian jostled a Muslim woman, then threw dust over her, seeking her for himself. That was reported to Umar ibn al-Khattab, so Umar said: These people have a covenant only so long as they fulfill your covenant to you; when they do not fulfill, they have no covenant. So Umar crucified him."
"18741","Al-Aslami reported from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, that a Muslim woman hired a Jew or a Christian, and he went with her. When they reached a mound, he hid with her behind it, then overpowered her. Abu Salih said: And I had watched her being overpowered when he overpowered her, so I struck him and did not leave him until I thought I had killed him. He went to Abu Hurayra and informed him. He said: So he summoned me, and I informed him, and he sent for the woman, and she agreed with me in the report. Abu Hurayra said: It is not upon this that we gave you the covenant. So he ordered him killed."
"18742","Ibn Jurayj reported: One whom I trust informed me that a Jew or a Christian jostled a Muslim woman and she fell. So Umar ibn al-Khattab struck his neck and said: It is not upon this that we made terms with you."
"18743","Ibn Jurayj reported: I was informed that Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah likewise killed a man who sought a woman for herself, and Abu Hurayra likewise. That was because a man of the People of the Book wanted to wrest a Muslim woman away for herself, while a man watched. So Abu Hurayra asked the man where the Muslim woman could not hear, and the Muslim woman where the man could not hear, and when the two agreed, he ordered his killing. And it was said to me: The man was Abu Salih al-Zayyat. He said: And Abd al-Malik gave the same judgment concerning a Bedouin slave-girl whom a man of the People of the Book deflowered: he killed him and gave the slave-girl his property."
"18744","Ibn Jurayj reported: Ibn Shihab said concerning a man of the People of the Book who secretly bought a Muslim slave-girl, and she bore him a child, he said: He is punished and she is taken away from him. Al-Thawri said: Concerning a dhimmi in whose possession a slave accepts Islam, and he conceals him or hides him, he said: He is disciplined and the slave is sold."
"18745","Ibn Jurayj reported: Ata said to me: Whoever steals wine from the People of the Book has his hand cut. Maamar and al-Thawri reported from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ata, the same."
"18746","Al-Hasan ibn Umara reported from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, he said: When a Christian slave accepts Islam, his owner is compelled to sell him."
"18747","Ibn al-Mubarak reported: Hakim ibn Ruzayq informed us that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to his father: To proceed, I have written to our governors not to leave a Muslim slave in the possession of a Christian but that he be taken and sold, nor a Muslim woman under a Christian but that they separate between them. So carry that out among those under you."
"18748","Ibn Jurayj reported: Ibn Shihab was asked about a Christian who had a Christian slave-girl of his, and she bore him a child, then she accepted Islam. He said: Islam separates between them, and she and her child are set free. He said: So I say: She is not set free until he is called to Islam; if he refuses to accept Islam, she is freed, and if he accepts Islam, she remains his slave-girl."
"18749","Ibn Mubarak reported: Harmala ibn Imran informed me that Ali ibn Taliq informed him that the slave-mother of a child of a Christian from Palestine accepted Islam, so he wrote about her to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, and he wrote: Send men to assess her value, and when her value is fixed, pay it to him from the public treasury, and let her go free, for she is a woman of the Muslims."
"18750","Ibn Jurayj reported: Khallad informed me that Amr ibn Shuayb informed him that Umar ibn al-Khattab would not let a Jew or a Christian Christianize or Judaize his child in the domain of the Arabs."
"18751","Ibn Jurayj reported from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Bajala al-Tamimi saying: I was a scribe for Jaz ibn Muawiya, the uncle of al-Ahnaf ibn Qays, and the letter of Umar came a year before his death: Kill every sorcerer, separate every closely related couple among the Magians, and forbid them the zamzama humming. He said: So we killed three sorceresses, and Jaz prepared much food and invited the Magians; so they cast away toothpicks they used to eat with, amounting to a mule-load or two mule-loads of silver, and they ate without humming."
"18752","He said: And he had not taken the jizya from the Magians until Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf testified that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it from the Magians of Hajar. Ibn Uyayna reported from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Bajala al-Tamimi narrating to Abu al-Shatha and Amr ibn Aws at the bench of Zamzam during the governorship of Musab ibn al-Zubayr, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Ibn Jurayj."
"18753","Ibn Uyayna reported from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani, from Kurdus al-Taghlibi, he said: A man of Banu Taghlib came to Umar, so Umar said to him: You had a share in the pre-Islamic age, so take your share of Islam. So he made terms with him that the jizya be doubled upon them, and that they not Christianize the children."
"18754","Ibn al-Taymi reported from Abu Awana, from al-Kalbi, from al-Asbagh ibn Nubata, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, he said: I witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he made terms with the Christians of Banu Taghlib that they not Christianize the children; if they do, they have no covenant. He said: And Ali said: Had I finished, I would have fought them."
"18755","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ismail and Yaqub and others, they said: Their sorcerer is not killed. That is because something of that was done to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not kill its doer, and he was of the people of the covenant. And the report of Jaz ibn Muawiya in the letter of Umar to him that a sorcerer be killed, and the report of Jundub when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: He strikes a blow distinguishing thereby between truth and falsehood; and in the indemnities there is a trick of the sorcerer. [source wording garbled at end]"
"18756","Maamar reported from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib and Urwa ibn al-Zubayr, that the Jews of Banu Zurayq bewitched the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and it is not mentioned that he killed any of them."
"18757","Al-Thawri reported from Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Ala, from Suwayd ibn Ghafla, he said: It reached Umar that his governors were taking wine as part of the jizya, so he adjured them three times. Bilal said: They do that. He said: Let them not do it, but let them handle its sale themselves; the Jews were forbidden fats, yet they sold them and consumed their prices."
"18758","Al-Thawri reported from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: When the people of the covenant pass with wine, the tithe-collector takes a tenth of it; he assesses its value, then takes a tenth of its value."
"18759","Al-Thawri reported from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, that he heard Ziyad ibn Hudayr saying: The first tithe-collector to collect tithes in Islam am I, and we would tithe neither a Muslim nor a covenanted person. I said: Whom did you tithe? He said: The Christians of Banu Taghlib. Ibrahim said: And a man narrated to me from Ziyad, he said: I said to him: How much did you tithe them? He said: Half a tenth."
"18760","Al-Thawri reported: Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid informed me, from Abd Allah ibn Mughaffal, that Ziyad ibn Hudayr narrated to him that he used to collect tithes in the governorship of Umar, tithing neither a Muslim nor a covenanted person. I said to him: Whom did you tithe? He said: The merchants of the people of war, as they tithe us when we come to them. He said: And Ziyad was a governor for Umar."
"18761","Abd Allah reported from Shuba, from al-Hakam ibn Utayba, he said: I heard Ibrahim narrating from Ziyad ibn Hudayr, and Ziyad was alive at that time, that Umar sent him as a collector of alms, and ordered him to take a tenth from the Christians of Banu Taghlib, and half a tenth from the Christians of the People of the Book."
"18762","Al-Thawri reported from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote concerning a dihqana woman of Nahr al-Malik who accepted Islam, and she had much land, and Umar was written to about her, so he wrote: Hand her land to her, she paying the kharaj upon it."
"18763","Al-Thawri reported from Jabir, from al-Shabi, that al-Rufayl, the dihqan of the two Nahr Karbala, accepted Islam, so Umar assigned him a stipend of two thousand, and handed him his land, he paying the kharaj upon it."
"18764","Hushaym ibn Bashir reported: Sayyar Abu al-Hakam informed me, from al-Zubayr ibn Adi, that Ali ibn Abi Talib said to a dihqan: If you accept Islam, I shall lift the dinar from your head."
"18765","Ibn Uyayna reported from Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Amr ibn Maymun al-Awdi, he said: I heard Umar, four days before his killing, while he stood upon his mount over Hudhayfa ibn al-Yaman and Uthman ibn Hunayf, saying: Look to what is before you both; let you not have burdened the land beyond what it can bear. Hudhayfa said: We burdened the land with a matter it can bear, and I left them the like of what I took from them. And Uthman ibn Hunayf said: I burdened the land with a matter it can bear, and I left them a small surplus. He said: Look to what is before you both; let you not have burdened the land beyond what it can bear, for if Allah keeps me safe, I shall leave the widows of Iraq needing no one after me."
"18766","Ibn Uyayna reported from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: Whatever city is conquered by force, its people are slaves and their property belongs to the Muslims; if they accept Islam before they are divided, they are free, but their property belongs to the Muslims."
"18767","Al-Thawri said: Whoever of the people of the covenant is in need and finds nothing to pay for his jizya, he said: He is given respite until he finds and pays, and nothing else is upon him; if he becomes solvent, what is past is taken; if he is unable to meet anything of the settlement he made terms upon, it is lifted from him when his inability is known; the Imam lifts it from him."
"18768","Ibn Jurayj reported: Sulayman al-Ahwal informed me, from Tawus, he said: When two jizyas accumulate upon a man, the first is taken."
"18769","Abd Allah reported from Shuba, from Muawiya Abu Abd Allah al-Asqalani, he said: One who saw Abd Allah ibn Muhayriz informed me: He shook hands with a Christian man in Damascus."
"18770","Al-Hasan ibn Umara reported from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to dislike eating with Jews and Christians, and shaking hands."
"18771","Maamar reported from al-Zuhri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave Safwan ibn Umayya a kunya while he was at that time a polytheist; he came to him upon a horse, and he said: Dismount, Abu Wahb."
"18772","Al-Thawri reported from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, that Umar gave al-Furafisa al-Hanafi, while he was a Christian, a kunya, saying to him: Abu Hassan. Ibn Uyayna reported from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Umar, the same."
"18773","Maamar narrated from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: This is the judgment of Muadh ibn Jabal concerning those whom Allah set free of the mustahmi of Himyar [source text partly lacunose]: Whoever grants protection to a people, or free men and weak neighbors belong to them, then to the one granted belongs what [lacuna] in his house until Islam entered. Whoever was neglected, paying the kharaj, is free. Whoever was bought or taken as spoil from the enemy of the religion, none of them testifying against another in fighting, belongs to the one who bought or captured him. Whoever brings a clear jizya or a clear ransom is free. Whoever withdrew his hand from his master in the pre-Islamic age, then was not seized until Islam entered, is free. Whoever withdrew his hand in peace to the Muslims while his master was a disbeliever is free. Whoever had land is most entitled to it; it is his land and his fathers land; it is his grant, not taken from him until Islam entered; to him belongs what he accepted Islam upon of it while it is under him. Whoever had land, or his fathers, or land was gifted to him and he consumed it until Islam entered, it is his. Whoever granted land that is not the grantees land, it belongs to the granter. Every loaned thing is returned to its owner. Every tiller of land who accepted Islam upon it is not removed from it so long as he gives its owner its share: a quarter of the irrigated and a tenth of the rain-fed, unless he seeks its sale, in which case he offers it to its tiller for a price; if he does not sell it, let him sell it to whom he wishes. Whoever goes to a district other than the district of his unirrigated crop, his tithes are charity to the chief of his clan. Whoever pledges land as security, the pledge-holder reckons its produce from the year of the pilgrimage of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until his death. Whoever had a slave-girl known to be his, and none overpowered him for her in the pre-Islamic age until he accepted Islam, without innovating, she belongs to her master. Whoever cultivates ownerless land in the pre-Islamic age until Islam entered, and it was not a grant, whoever consumed it until Islam entered without paying a due upon it, it is his. Whoever buys land with his wealth, it is his. Whoever gives a woman a dower, to her belongs his dower. Whoever gives his wife slaves as dower, or free men belonging to them, and gives them to her: if she took them out of their families, they belong to her, and if she did not take them out of their families and the first of them are free, to her belong twelve ounces of gold, and they are freed. Whoever grants land on condition that he be heard and obeyed and served, it belongs to the one to whom it was granted, if he consumed it until Islam entered. Whoever grants land to a man until he is pleased or secured by it, it belongs to the one to whom he granted it. This is the judgment of Muadh, and the commander is Abu Bakr."
"18774","He said: Maamar informed us, from Ayyub, that he took this document from Amr ibn Dinar. This is what Ali ibn Abi Talib acknowledged and decreed concerning his property: He gave Yanbu in charity seeking the pleasure of Allah, that He admit him to Paradise and turn the Fire away from him. It is in the path of Allah and for His countenance, spent in every spending of the path of Allah and His countenance: in war and peace, for good, kindred, near and far. It is not sold, nor given away, nor inherited; all property in Yanbu, except that Rabah and Abu Nizar and Jubayr, if something happens to me, have no claim upon them; they are freed clients working in the property for five pilgrimages, with their maintenance and provision therein, and the provision of their families. That is what I decree concerning what was mine in Yanbu, whether I am alive or dead, along with what was mine in Wadi Umm al-Qura of property and slaves, alive or dead, along with al-Udhayna and its people, alive or dead, along with Rad and its people, except that Zurayq is like what I wrote for Abu Nizar and Rabah and Jubayr. And Yanbu and what is in Wadi al-Qura, al-Udhayna, and Rad are spent in every spending seeking thereby the countenance of Allah in His path, on the day when faces turn black and faces turn white. They are not sold, nor given away, nor inherited, except to Allah; He accepts them and He inherits them. That is a decree between me and Allah from tomorrow of the day I came to Maskan, alive or dead. This is what Ali decreed concerning his property, binding and conclusive. Then the sons of Ali stand over that in trust and uprightness, as they uprightly manage their own property: it is cultivated and managed as they manage their property. None of the villages of the sons of Ali of these four villages shall have a single wadi sold, while its land is covered by its planting, its cultivation standing for the believers, first and last. Whoever of the people takes charge of it, I remind him by Allah but to strive and advise and guard his trust. This is the document of Ali ibn Abi Talib in his own hand when he came to Maskan. And I enjoined [lacuna] the two poor in the path of Allah, binding and conclusive. And the property of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is apart, spent in the path of Allah and His countenance, for kindred, the poor, the destitute, and the wayfarer; its workers eat from it in a proper manner without wrongdoing, in trust and uprightness, as one uprightly manages his own property: cultivating, advising, striving. This is what Ali ibn Abi Talib decreed concerning these properties written in this sheet, and Allah is the One sought for help in every state. To proceed, my slave-girls whom I visit, nineteen in number, among them mothers of children whose children are alive with them, among them pregnant ones, and among them those with no child: I decree, if something happens to me in this campaign, that whoever of them has no child and is not pregnant is free for the countenance of Allah, with no claim upon her for anyone; and whoever of them is pregnant or has a child is kept with her child, and she is of his share; if her child dies while she is alive, no one has a claim upon her. This is what I decreed concerning my nineteen slave-girls. Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Rafi and Hayyaj ibn Abi Hayyaj witnessed, and Ali wrote in his hand: On ten nights past of Jumada al-Ula, in the year thirty-nine."
"18775","In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate: This is the document of Abd Allah Umar, Commander of the Believers, concerning Thamgh: that if he dies, it goes to Hafsa as long as she lives; she spends its produce where Allah shows her. If she dies, it goes to the one of sound judgment of her family, its root never to be bought nor given away. Whoever takes charge of it, there is no blame upon him in its produce, whether he eats or feeds a friend, without amassing wealth from it. What remains of its produce is for the beggar, the deprived, the guest, kindred, the wayfarer, and in the path of Allah; he spends it where Allah shows him of that. And if I die, and the hundred wasqs which Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fed me in the valley are in my hand, I have not destroyed them; they are with Thamgh upon the practice I commanded. If the guardian of Thamgh wishes, he buys slaves from its produce for its work. Muayqib wrote, and Abd Allah ibn al-Arqam witnessed."
"18776","In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate: This is what Abd Allah Umar, Commander of the Believers, enjoined, if something happens to him: that Thamgh and Sirma ibn al-Akwa are charity, and the slave in it, and the hundred shares in Khaybar, and its slaves therein, and the hundred which Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fed me: Hafsa is in charge of it as long as she lives, then the one of sound judgment of his family takes charge; it is not sold nor bought; he spends it where he sees: for the beggar, the deprived, and kindred. And there is no blame upon its guardian if he eats or feeds, or buys slaves from it."
"18777","In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate: This is what Amr ibn al-As decreed concerning al-Waht: He decreed that it is charity in the path of the charity which Allah commanded, upon the practice of the charities of the Muslims. He gave it in charity seeking the countenance of Allah and the Hereafter. It is not sold, nor given away, nor inherited, until Allah inherits it, standing upon its roots. None inherits it, and it is not permitted for anyone to change anything of what I decreed therein and enjoined. I make it inviolable with what Allah made inviolable of the property of the Muslims, their persons, and their charities. It is not sold, nor inherited, nor destroyed, nor is my decree therein which I decreed and left it upon changed. It is not lawful for a Muslim who worships Allah to alter anything of it, nor to change it from its covenant. What I assigned for it is entrusted to a guardian of the family of Amr ibn al-As, the upright guardian among them, not the corrupting, following therein my decree and covenant. Whoever wishes to diminish it or change anything of it is the foolish nullifier who has no decree in my charity nor command. I wrote this document only for fear that a fool [lacuna] by kinship, who does not know the matter of my charity and what I left it upon and enjoined therein, should entertain in himself what is not lawful for him, out of little knowledge and foolish judgment. None of those has any right in my charity nor command. I adjure by Allah every Muslim who worships Allah, of kin or other, and every Imam whom Allah entrusted with the affair of the Muslims, not to change my charity from what I enjoined therein or decreed and left it upon. Talha ibn Ubayd Allah, Mabad ibn Mamar, Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, Abu Jahm ibn Hudhayfa, al-Harith ibn al-Hakam, Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, Abd al-Rahman ibn Muti, Jubayr ibn al-Huwayrith, Abu Sufyan ibn Mahid, and Nafi ibn Tarif [witnessed]. It was written on ten nights past of Muharram, in the year twenty-nine."
